window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-cbse12-acc-admission","kind":"practice","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"title":"Admission of a Partner · Practice","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","subject":"Accountancy","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-001","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":1,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi and Kavi are partners. They admit Navi, who cannot bring his share of goodwill in cash. Navi takes his share of profit from Ravi and Kavi in a ratio that is different from their old ratio. No goodwill account exists in the books. In which ratio will Ravi's and Kavi's capital accounts be credited for Navi's share of goodwill?","image":"","options":["Old profit sharing ratio","Sacrificing ratio","New profit sharing ratio","Equally"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: Goodwill is paid to compensate the old partners for the share of profit they give up.\nStep 2: Each old partner should therefore be credited in proportion to what he gave up, which is the sacrificing ratio.\nStep 3: The entry is: New Partner's Current A/c Dr. To Sacrificing Partners' Capital A/cs.\nStep 4: The old ratio applies only when nothing is said about the sacrifice. Here the sacrifice is stated to be different from the old ratio, so the old ratio does not apply.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-002","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":2,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following features of a business firm:\n(i) It is located in a busy market area with heavy customer traffic.\n(ii) It faces very limited competition in its market.\n(iii) It holds patents and long-term contracts for the supply of materials.\n(iv) It earns only the normal rate of return on its capital employed.\n(v) It has efficient management, which gives high productivity at low cost.\nHow many of the above features would tend to increase the value of the firm's goodwill?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Increases goodwill: a favourable location brings more customers.\n(ii) Increases goodwill: a monopoly-like market lets the firm earn higher profits.\n(iii) Increases goodwill: patents and long-term contracts are special advantages.\n(iv) Does not increase goodwill: goodwill exists only when a firm earns super profits. A firm earning just normal profits has no goodwill.\n(v) Increases goodwill: efficient management leads to higher profits.\nSo 4 features increase goodwill.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-003","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":3,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Pooja and Qadir share profits in the ratio of 3:2. They admit Rhea for 1/5 share in profits. The new profit sharing ratio of Pooja, Qadir and Rhea is 5:3:2. Rhea brings Rs. 24,000 as premium for goodwill in cash. What amount of the premium will be credited to Qadir's capital account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 9,600","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 14,400","Rs. 7,200"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: old shares are Pooja $\\frac{6}{10}$ and Qadir $\\frac{4}{10}$. New shares are $\\frac{5}{10}$ and $\\frac{3}{10}$. The premium is Rs. 24,000.\nPooja's sacrifice = $\\frac{6}{10}-\\frac{5}{10}=\\frac{1}{10}$.\nQadir's sacrifice = $\\frac{4}{10}-\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{1}{10}$.\nThe sacrificing ratio is therefore 1:1, and the premium is shared in this ratio.\nQadir gets $24{,}000\\times\\frac{1}{2}$ = Rs. 12,000.\nRs. 9,600 is wrong because it splits the premium in the old ratio, which is not the ratio of sacrifice here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-004","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":4,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: P and Q share profits equally. Their balance sheet shows:\nLiabilities: Creditors Rs. 30,000; General Reserve Rs. 10,000; Capitals P Rs. 50,000, Q Rs. 40,000.\nAssets: Cash Rs. 20,000; Debtors Rs. 30,000; Stock Rs. 30,000; Furniture Rs. 50,000.\nR is admitted on the following terms:\n(i) R brings Rs. 40,000 as capital in cash.\n(ii) Furniture is reduced by Rs. 5,000.\n(iii) A provision of 5% is created on debtors.\n(iv) Outstanding rent of Rs. 2,000 is unrecorded.\nWhat will be the total of the balance sheet of the reconstituted firm?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,63,500","Rs. 1,70,000","Rs. 1,61,500","Rs. 1,65,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1, revaluation loss: furniture 5,000 + provision 1,500 + rent 2,000 = Rs. 8,500. This is shared equally by P and Q, Rs. 4,250 each.\nStep 2, general reserve: Rs. 10,000 is transferred to P and Q, Rs. 5,000 each. It no longer appears as a liability.\nStep 3, assets: cash 60,000 + debtors (30,000 − 1,500) 28,500 + stock 30,000 + furniture 45,000 = Rs. 1,63,500.\nStep 4, liabilities check: creditors 30,000 + outstanding rent 2,000 + capitals P 50,750, Q 40,750, R 40,000 = Rs. 1,63,500.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-005","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":5,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Xavier and Yamini share profits in the ratio of 3:2. Zoya is admitted for $\\frac{1}{5}$ share in profits, which she acquires wholly from Xavier. What is Xavier's new share of profit?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2}{5}$","$\\frac{12}{25}$","$\\frac{3}{5}$","$\\frac{1}{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Xavier's old share = $\\frac{3}{5}$. Zoya's share of $\\frac{1}{5}$ comes wholly from Xavier.\nFormula: New share = Old share − Share surrendered.\nSubstituting: Xavier = $\\frac{3}{5} - \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{2}{5}$. Yamini keeps $\\frac{2}{5}$, so the new ratio is 2:2:1.\nThe value $\\frac{12}{25}$ would apply only if Zoya acquired her share in the old ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-006","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":6,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"X and Y share profits in the ratio 3:2. After all adjustments, their capitals are Rs. 70,000 and Rs. 50,000 respectively. Z is admitted for 1/4 share in profits and brings Rs. 60,000 as capital. He does not bring any amount for goodwill, and no goodwill appears in the books.\nConsider the statements:\n(i) The hidden goodwill of the firm is Rs. 60,000.\n(ii) Z's Current A/c will be debited with Rs. 15,000.\n(iii) X's Capital A/c will be credited with Rs. 9,000.\n(iv) Z's Capital A/c will be debited with Rs. 15,000 for his share of goodwill.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Total capital implied by Z's contribution = 60,000 × 4 = Rs. 2,40,000.\nActual combined capital = 70,000 + 50,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 1,80,000. So hidden goodwill = Rs. 60,000.\n(i) Correct.\n(ii) Correct: Z's share = 1/4 × 60,000 = Rs. 15,000, debited to his current account.\n(iii) Correct: the sacrifice is in the old ratio 3:2, so X gets 3/5 × 15,000 = Rs. 9,000.\n(iv) Incorrect: NCERT debits unpaid goodwill to the new partner's current account, not his capital account.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-007","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":7,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"M and N share profits in the ratio 2:1. Their capitals after all adjustments are Rs. 90,000 and Rs. 60,000. O is admitted for 1/4 share, which he acquires entirely from N. O brings Rs. 70,000 as capital but nothing for goodwill, and goodwill is not given. How much will be credited to N's capital account for goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Implied total capital = 70,000 × 4 = Rs. 2,80,000.\nActual total capital = 90,000 + 60,000 + 70,000 = Rs. 2,20,000.\nHidden goodwill = 2,80,000 − 2,20,000 = Rs. 60,000; O's share = 60,000 × 1/4 = Rs. 15,000.\nOnly N sacrifices, so the entry is: O's Current A/c Dr. 15,000 To N's Capital A/c 15,000. M gets nothing.\nRs. 5,000 is the wrong split, made by wrongly using the old ratio 2:1.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-008","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":8,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Mohan and Sohan are partners sharing profits in the ratio 4:3. They admit Rohan as a new partner. Mohan surrenders $\\frac{1}{4}$ of his share and Sohan surrenders $\\frac{1}{3}$ of his share in favour of Rohan. Rohan brings Rs. 21,000 as premium for goodwill, which is passed through the firm's books.\nWhat amount of premium will be credited to Mohan's capital account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 12,000","Rs. 10,500","Rs. 9,000","Rs. 13,500"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mohan's sacrifice = $\\frac{1}{4}\\times\\frac{4}{7}=\\frac{1}{7}$.\nSohan's sacrifice = $\\frac{1}{3}\\times\\frac{3}{7}=\\frac{1}{7}$.\nThe sacrificing ratio is therefore 1:1, and the new ratio is 3:2:2.\nMohan's credit = $21{,}000\\times\\frac{1}{2}$ = Rs. 10,500.\nRs. 12,000 wrongly divides the premium in the old ratio 4:3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-009","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":9,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"P, Q and R share profits in the ratio 4:3:1. From the new financial year they decide to share profits equally. The firm's goodwill is valued at Rs. 1,20,000 and is not to appear in the books. By how much will P's capital account be credited in the goodwill adjustment entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 25,000","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Old shares in 24ths: P = 12/24, Q = 9/24, R = 3/24. New shares: 8/24 each.\nP sacrifices 4/24 and Q sacrifices 1/24, while R gains 5/24.\nThe gaining partner compensates the sacrificing partners. The entry is R's Capital Dr. 25,000 (1,20,000 × 5/24), To P's Capital 20,000 and To Q's Capital 5,000.\nSo P is credited 1,20,000 × 4/24 = Rs. 20,000.\nRs. 25,000 is wrong because that is R's debit, not P's credit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-010","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":10,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Kabir and Leena share profits in the ratio 3:2. On admitting Mira, Kabir's share in the new firm becomes $\\frac{1}{3}$. What fraction of profit has Kabir sacrificed?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2}{15}$","$\\frac{4}{15}$","$\\frac{8}{15}$","$\\frac{1}{15}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Kabir's old share = $\\frac{3}{5}$ and his new share = $\\frac{1}{3}$.\nFormula: Sacrifice = Old share − New share.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{9-5}{15}$.\nSo Kabir's sacrifice = $\\frac{4}{15}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-011","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":11,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran and Leela share profits in the ratio 3:2. From April 1, 2024 they decide to share profits equally. What share of profit does Kiran sacrifice?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{10}$","$\\frac{1}{5}$","$\\frac{3}{10}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Kiran's old share = $\\frac{3}{5}$ and his new share = $\\frac{1}{2}$.\nFormula: Sacrifice = Old share − New share.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{3}{5} - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{6-5}{10} = \\frac{1}{10}$.\nLeela gains the same $\\frac{1}{10}$, because her share rises from $\\frac{2}{5}$ to $\\frac{1}{2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-012","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":12,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a new partner is admitted, goodwill already appearing in the books is written off by debiting the old partners' capital accounts in their old profit sharing ratio.\nReason (R): Goodwill existing at the time of admission was earned before the new partner joined, so it belongs only to the old partners.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT writes off existing goodwill against old partners' capitals in the old ratio. This is in line with AS 26.\nR is true. Goodwill built up before admission belongs to the old partners.\nBecause the old partners own that goodwill, they alone bear its write-off, and in the ratio in which they owned it. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-013","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":13,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A partner newly admitted to the firm has no claim on the balance of General Reserve that appears in the books on the date of his admission.\nReason (R): Reserves are profits earned and kept aside before the admission, so they belong to the old partners and are transferred to their capital accounts in the old profit sharing ratio.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the new partner is not entitled to any share in accumulated profits such as general reserve, reserve or Profit and Loss balance.\nR is true. These profits were earned when only the old partners were in the firm. So they are credited to the old partners' capital or current accounts in the old ratio.\nR gives the reason why the new partner has no claim on them, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-014","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":14,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Purchased goodwill may be shown as an asset. If it is written off over more than one accounting year, the period should not exceed 10 years.\nStatement II: If self-generated goodwill is debited to Goodwill Account, it should be written off in the same financial year and not shown as an asset in the balance sheet.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Under AS 26, purchased goodwill may be recorded and shown as an asset. It should be written off as early as possible, and where it is written off over more than one year, within a period not exceeding 10 years.\nStatement II is correct. Self-generated goodwill cannot appear as an asset. If it is raised in the books, it must be written off in the same financial year.\nThis is also why goodwill already appearing in the books is written off when a firm is reconstituted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-015","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":15,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"The profits of a firm for the last four years were: 2020–21 Rs. 60,000; 2021–22 Rs. 80,000; 2022–23 Loss Rs. 20,000; 2023–24 Rs. 1,00,000. Goodwill is to be valued at 3 years' purchase of the average profits of the last four years. The value of goodwill is:","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,65,000","Rs. 1,95,000","Rs. 1,80,000","Rs. 2,40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Profits are 60,000, 80,000, (20,000) and 1,00,000. Goodwill = 3 years' purchase.\nFormula: Average profit = Net total ÷ number of years. Goodwill = Average profit × years' purchase.\nA loss year is subtracted. It is not ignored and not added. Net total = 60,000 + 80,000 − 20,000 + 1,00,000 = Rs. 2,20,000.\nAverage profit = 2,20,000 ÷ 4 = Rs. 55,000.\nGoodwill = 55,000 × 3 = Rs. 1,65,000.\nRs. 1,95,000 is wrong because it treats the loss as a profit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-016","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":16,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (item on admission of a partner) with List-II (accounting treatment):\nList-I\n(A) General reserve in the old balance sheet\n(B) Premium for goodwill brought in cash by the new partner\n(C) Share of goodwill not brought in cash by the new partner\n(D) Capitals to be made proportionate, as agreed\nList-II\n(i) Debited to the new partner's current account\n(ii) Credited to old partners in sacrificing ratio\n(iii) Based on the new profit sharing ratio\n(iv) Credited to old partners in old profit sharing ratio","image":"","options":["A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) General reserve is an accumulated profit, so it goes to old partners in the old ratio: (iv).\n(B) Premium compensates the partners who sacrifice, so it is shared in the sacrificing ratio: (ii).\n(C) Goodwill the new partner does not bring is debited to his current account: (i).\n(D) Adjusted capitals are kept proportionate to the new profit sharing ratio: (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-017","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":17,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"On the admission of a partner, the following adjustments are made:\n(i) Stock reduced in value\n(ii) Creditors increased due to an omitted bill\n(iii) Unrecorded investment brought into books\n(iv) Provision for doubtful debts created on debtors\n(v) A creditor not likely to claim is written off\n(vi) Outstanding electricity bill provided for\nHow many of these are debited to the Revaluation Account?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Losses are debited to the Revaluation Account and gains are credited to it.\n(i) Debit: a decrease in an asset is a loss.\n(ii) Debit: an increase in a liability is a loss.\n(iii) Credit: an unrecorded asset is a gain.\n(iv) Debit: creating a provision for doubtful debts reduces the value of debtors, which is a loss.\n(v) Credit: a decrease in a liability is a gain.\n(vi) Debit: an unrecorded liability is a loss.\nItems (i), (ii), (iv) and (vi) are debited, so the count is 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-018","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":18,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation on admission of a partner) with List-II (treatment in the firm's books):\nList-I\n(a) New partner pays premium directly (privately) to the old partners\n(b) Premium credited to Premium for Goodwill A/c is distributed\n(c) Old partners withdraw the premium credited to them\n(d) Goodwill already appearing in the books is written off\nList-II\n(i) No journal entry in the books of the firm\n(ii) Old Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. To Goodwill A/c (old ratio)\n(iii) Premium for Goodwill A/c Dr. To Sacrificing Partners' Capital A/cs (sacrificing ratio)\n(iv) Old Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. To Bank A/c","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(i): A private payment does not pass through the firm, so no entry is made.\n(b)-(iii): The premium is transferred to the sacrificing partners in their sacrificing ratio.\n(c)-(iv): A withdrawal reduces capital and bank, so Capital A/cs are debited and Bank A/c is credited.\n(d)-(ii): Existing goodwill is written off against the old partners' capitals in the old ratio. It is not written off in the sacrificing ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-019","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":19,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following items will appear on the credit side of the Revaluation Account at the time of admission of a partner?\n(i) Increase in the value of machinery\n(ii) Investment not recorded in the books, now taken into account\n(iii) A creditor not likely to claim his amount, written off\n(iv) Creation of a provision for doubtful debts\n(v) Outstanding salary not recorded earlier\n(vi) Decrease in the value of stock","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The credit side of the Revaluation Account records gains; the debit side records losses.\n(i) Credit: increase in an asset is a gain.\n(ii) Credit: an unrecorded asset is a gain.\n(iii) Credit: a decrease in a liability is a gain.\n(iv) Debit: creating a provision is a loss.\n(v) Debit: an unrecorded liability is a loss.\n(vi) Debit: a decrease in an asset is a loss.\nSo 3 items appear on the credit side.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-020","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":20,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The premium for goodwill brought in by a new partner is shared among the old partners in their sacrificing ratio.\nReason (R): The sacrificing ratio is always the same as the old profit sharing ratio.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The premium compensates old partners for the share of profit they give up, so it is divided in the ratio of sacrifice.\nR is false. The sacrificing ratio equals the old ratio only when the new partner acquires his share in the old ratio. It can differ, for example with equal sacrifice, a specified ratio, or a sacrifice worked out from a given new ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-021","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":21,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Which two main rights does a newly admitted partner acquire in a partnership firm?","image":"","options":["Right to manage the firm alone and right to share profits","Right to share the assets and right to share the profits of the firm","Right to share the profits and right to dissolve the firm at will","Right to share the assets and right to receive a fixed salary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT states that a newly admitted partner acquires two main rights.\n(i) The right to share the assets of the partnership firm.\n(ii) The right to share the profits of the partnership firm.\nSole management, dissolution at will and a fixed salary are not rights that come automatically with admission.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-022","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":22,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Ria and Sam are partners with total capital of ₹2,00,000. The normal rate of return is 12%. Each partner is entitled to an annual salary of ₹5,000. The firm's profits for the last three years were ₹40,000, ₹46,000 and ₹52,000. Goodwill is to be valued at 2 years' purchase of average super profits. What is the value of goodwill?","image":"","options":["₹24,000","₹44,000","₹34,000","₹1,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Capital = ₹2,00,000; rate = 12%; salary = ₹5,000 × 2 partners; profits ₹40,000, ₹46,000 and ₹52,000.\nNormal profit = interest on capital + partners' salary = 24,000 + 10,000 = ₹34,000.\nAverage profit = 1,38,000/3 = ₹46,000.\nSuper profit = 46,000 − 34,000 = ₹12,000.\nGoodwill = 12,000 × 2 = ₹24,000.\nWhy not ₹44,000: that answer ignores the partners' salaries, which must be included in normal profit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-023","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":23,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Kiran and Lata share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Om, who brings Rs. 18,000 as premium for goodwill. It is agreed that Kiran and Lata will sacrifice in the ratio 1:2. What amount of premium will be credited to Kiran's capital account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 10,800","Rs. 9,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 6,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: premium = Rs. 18,000 and sacrificing ratio = 1:2.\nRule: premium is credited to the old partners in their sacrificing ratio, not in the old ratio.\nSubstituting: Kiran's share = $18{,}000 \\times \\frac{1}{3}$.\nSo Kiran is credited Rs. 6,000.\nRs. 10,800 is the tempting wrong option because it uses the old ratio 3:2.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-024","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":24,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Arun and Bela admit Chetan into their firm for $\\frac{1}{5}$ share in future profits. Chetan brings Rs. 50,000 as his capital. The partners agree that Chetan's capital will be the base for fixing the total capital of the reconstituted firm. What is the total capital of the new firm?","image":"","options":["Rs. 2,50,000","Rs. 2,00,000","Rs. 3,00,000","Rs. 1,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Chetan's capital = Rs. 50,000 for a $\\frac{1}{5}$ share.\nFormula: Total capital = New partner's capital × reciprocal of his share.\nSubstituting: 50,000 × $\\frac{5}{1}$ = Rs. 2,50,000.\nRs. 2,00,000 is wrong because it is only the old partners' combined capital, i.e. the $\\frac{4}{5}$ portion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-025","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":25,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"X and Y share profits in the ratio 3:2. Goodwill appears in their books at Rs. 20,000. They admit Z for 1/5 share, which Z acquires equally from X and Y. The firm's goodwill is now valued at Rs. 50,000, and Z brings nothing for goodwill. After writing off the existing goodwill and adjusting Z's share of goodwill, by how much does X's capital account decrease (net)?","image":"","options":["Rs. 7,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 3,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write-off of existing goodwill (old ratio 3:2): X is debited 20,000 × 3/5 = Rs. 12,000.\nZ's share of goodwill = 50,000 × 1/5 = Rs. 10,000. This is debited to Z's current A/c.\nThe sacrifice is equal, so X is credited 10,000 × 1/2 = Rs. 5,000.\nNet effect on X = 12,000 − 5,000 = Rs. 7,000 decrease.\nRs. 6,000 results from wrongly using 3:2 for the credit; Rs. 3,000 is Y's net decrease.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-026","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":26,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Arun and Varun share profits in the ratio 3:1. Tarun is admitted and brings Rs. 12,000 in cash as premium for goodwill. Nothing is said about how Tarun acquires his share. What amount is credited to Arun's Capital A/c on distribution of the premium?","image":"","options":["Rs. 3,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 9,000","Rs. 12,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Premium = Rs. 12,000; old ratio = 3:1.\nRule: The premium is shared in the sacrificing ratio. When nothing is specified, the sacrificing ratio equals the old ratio, 3:1.\nSubstituting: Arun's share = $12,000 \\times \\frac{3}{4}$.\nSo Arun is credited with Rs. 9,000, and Varun with Rs. 3,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-027","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":27,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following events does NOT amount to reconstitution of a partnership firm?","image":"","options":["The existing partners decide to share future profits equally instead of in the ratio 3:2:1","A partner retires because of ill health and the others continue the business","The firm sells an old delivery van and buys a new one","A new partner is admitted with a 1/6 share in profits"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Reconstitution happens when the existing agreement among partners changes. Examples are admission, a change in the profit sharing ratio, retirement and death.\nOptions 1, 2 and 4 each change the partnership agreement, so each one reconstitutes the firm.\nSelling and replacing a van is an ordinary business transaction. It does not change the relationship among partners.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-028","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":28,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A, B and C share profits in the ratio 2:2:1. They decide to share profits equally in future. The firm's goodwill is valued at Rs. 1,50,000 and is not to appear in the books. By what amount will C's capital account be debited for the goodwill adjustment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 20,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 50,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Old shares are $\\frac{2}{5}, \\frac{2}{5}, \\frac{1}{5}$; new shares are $\\frac{1}{3}$ each.\nC's gain = $\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{2}{15}$. A and B each sacrifice $\\frac{2}{5} - \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{1}{15}$.\nC's debit = $1,50,000 \\times \\frac{2}{15}$ = Rs. 20,000.\nA and B are credited Rs. 10,000 each. Rs. 50,000 would wrongly charge C's whole new share of goodwill.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-029","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":29,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): At the time of admitting a new partner, the balance of General Reserve shown in the old Balance Sheet is credited to the old partners' capital accounts in their old profit sharing ratio.\nReason (R): The incoming partner has no right to share profits that the firm earned and set aside before he became a partner.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT requires reserves and accumulated profits to be transferred to the old partners' capital or current accounts in the old ratio.\nR is true: these profits were earned before the admission, so the new partner is not entitled to any share of them.\nR is the reason why the reserve goes only to the old partners, and why it is shared in the old ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-030","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":30,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"X and Y are partners. After all adjustments, their capitals are Rs. 70,000 and Rs. 50,000. Z is admitted for a 1/4 share in profits and brings Rs. 60,000 as capital. The value of goodwill is not given. What is the hidden goodwill of the firm?","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 2,40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Z brings Rs. 60,000 for a 1/4 share.\nImplied total capital of the new firm = 60,000 × 4 = Rs. 2,40,000.\nActual combined capital = 70,000 + 50,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nHidden goodwill = 2,40,000 − 1,80,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nRs. 15,000 is only Z's 1/4 share of this goodwill, not the firm's total goodwill.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-031","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":31,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"According to AS 26 (Intangible Assets), which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Internally generated goodwill should be recognised as an asset in the balance sheet.","Internally generated goodwill should not be recognised as an asset.","Purchased goodwill can never be shown in the books.","Intangible assets, once recorded, must never be written off."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: AS 26 states that internally generated (self-generated) goodwill should not be recognised as an asset.\n(2) Correct: this is the AS 26 rule itself.\n(3) Incorrect: purchased goodwill may be recorded in the books and shown as an asset.\n(4) Incorrect: intangible assets must be written off over their estimated life. This period normally should not exceed 10 years.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-032","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":32,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Anil, Bina and Chandan are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 4:3:2. They admit Divya. Anil surrenders $\\frac{1}{4}$ of his share, Bina surrenders $\\frac{1}{3}$ of her share and Chandan surrenders $\\frac{1}{2}$ of his share in favour of Divya. What is the new profit sharing ratio of Anil, Bina, Chandan and Divya?","image":"","options":["3 : 2 : 1 : 3","4 : 3 : 2 : 3","3 : 2 : 1 : 1","12 : 9 : 6 : 13"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Old shares are $\\frac{4}{9}, \\frac{3}{9}, \\frac{2}{9}$.\nSacrifices: Anil $\\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{4}{9} = \\frac{1}{9}$, Bina $\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{3}{9} = \\frac{1}{9}$, Chandan $\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{2}{9} = \\frac{1}{9}$.\nNew shares: Anil $\\frac{3}{9}$, Bina $\\frac{2}{9}$, Chandan $\\frac{1}{9}$.\nDivya = total sacrifice = $\\frac{3}{9}$, so the ratio is 3:2:1:3.\nOption (3) wrongly gives Divya only one partner's sacrifice. Option (2) leaves the old shares unreduced.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-033","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":33,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"At the time of admission of a new partner, the partners agree to adjust the old partners' capitals in the new profit sharing ratio. They will do this by opening current accounts and not through cash. One old partner's adjusted capital is more than the capital required under the new ratio. Which journal entry records this excess?","image":"","options":["Partner's Current A/c Dr. To Partner's Capital A/c","Partner's Capital A/c Dr. To Partner's Current A/c","Partner's Capital A/c Dr. To Cash A/c","Cash A/c Dr. To Partner's Capital A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Step 1: The excess capital has to be taken out of the capital account. So the capital account is debited.\nStep 2: The partners agreed to adjust through current accounts. So the excess is credited to the same partner's current account and no cash moves.\nStep 3: Option (1) is the entry for a deficiency, where the shortfall is debited to the current account.\nStep 4: Option (3) records a cash withdrawal, which is used only when the excess is actually withdrawn.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-034","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":34,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Amit and Bimal share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Charu for $\\frac{1}{4}$ share, which she acquires from them in their old ratio. Charu brings Rs. 40,000 as capital, and the old partners' capitals are to be adjusted on the basis of her capital. After all adjustments, Amit's capital is Rs. 1,00,000 and Bimal's capital is Rs. 50,000. The surplus is to be withdrawn in cash. How much will Amit withdraw?","image":"","options":["Rs. 28,000","Rs. 4,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 72,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Charu's capital = Rs. 40,000 for a $\\frac{1}{4}$ share.\nTotal capital = 40,000 × 4 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nNew shares: Amit = $\\frac{3}{5}$ × $\\frac{3}{4}$ = $\\frac{9}{20}$; Bimal = $\\frac{6}{20}$; Charu = $\\frac{5}{20}$.\nAmit's required capital = 1,60,000 × $\\frac{9}{20}$ = Rs. 72,000.\nAmit's withdrawal = 1,00,000 − 72,000 = Rs. 28,000.\nRs. 4,000 is wrong because it wrongly applies Amit's old share of $\\frac{3}{5}$ to the total capital.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-035","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":35,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A newly admitted partner gets no share in the general reserve appearing in the balance sheet on the date of his admission.\nReason (R): Accumulated profits such as general reserve were earned before his admission. They are transferred to the old partners' capital accounts in their old profit sharing ratio.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The new partner is not entitled to any share in accumulated profits.\nR is true. Reserves and profits not yet transferred belong to the period before admission, so they go to the old partners' capital or current accounts in the old ratio.\nR explains why the new partner gets no share in A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-036","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":36,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about goodwill brought in cash by a new partner are correct?\n(i) The premium is shared among the old partners in their sacrificing ratio.\n(ii) When the premium is paid privately to the old partners, Premium for Goodwill A/c is still credited in the firm's books.\n(iii) Goodwill already in the books is written off by debiting all partners, including the new partner, in the new ratio.\n(iv) When old partners withdraw their premium, their Capital A/cs are debited and Bank A/c is credited.\n(v) When the partners decide to retain the premium in the business, no additional entry is passed.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the premium compensates the partners who sacrificed, in the ratio of their sacrifice.\n(ii) Incorrect: a private payment needs no entry in the firm's books.\n(iii) Incorrect: only the old partners bear the write-off, in the old ratio.\n(iv) Correct: a withdrawal reduces capital and cash.\n(v) Correct: the premium stays credited in the capital accounts.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-037","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":37,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Asha and Rekha are partners with a total capital of Rs. 1,50,000. The normal rate of return is 12%. A fair annual remuneration for each partner's services is Rs. 8,000. The firm's profits for the last three years were Rs. 40,000, Rs. 44,000 and Rs. 51,000. Goodwill is to be valued at 2 years' purchase of the average super profits. What is the value of goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 54,000","Rs. 38,000","Rs. 11,000","Rs. 22,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Capital = Rs. 1,50,000; Rate = 12%; Salary = Rs. 8,000 × 2 partners; 2 years' purchase.\nStep 1 (normal profit): 1,50,000 × 12/100 = 18,000. Add salaries of 16,000. Normal profit = Rs. 34,000.\nStep 2 (average profit): (40,000 + 44,000 + 51,000)/3 = Rs. 45,000.\nStep 3 (super profit): 45,000 − 34,000 = Rs. 11,000.\nStep 4 (goodwill): 11,000 × 2 = Rs. 22,000.\nRs. 54,000 is wrong because it ignores the partners' salary.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-038","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":38,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the same data, goodwill calculated by capitalising the average profits is equal to goodwill calculated by capitalising the super profits.\nReason (R): When normal profit is capitalised at the normal rate of return, the result equals the capital employed (net assets) of the firm.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that both capitalisation methods give exactly the same goodwill.\nR is true. Normal profit = Capital × r/100. Capitalising it gives Normal profit × 100/r = Capital.\nR explains A. Capitalised average profit − Capital = (Average − Normal) × 100/r, which is the capitalised super profit.\nExample from NCERT data: ₹10,00,000 − ₹8,20,000 = ₹18,000 × 100/10 = ₹1,80,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-039","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":39,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun and Bela are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 5:3. They admit Chirag as a new partner for 1/4 share in the future profits. Nothing is agreed about how Chirag acquires his share from the old partners. What is the new profit sharing ratio of Arjun, Bela and Chirag?","image":"","options":["15:9:8","5:3:8","13:11:8","5:3:2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Old ratio 5:3. Chirag's share = 1/4. No sacrificing ratio is specified, so assume he acquires his share in the old ratio.\nRemaining share = 1 − 1/4 = 3/4.\nArjun = 5/8 × 3/4 = 15/32; Bela = 3/8 × 3/4 = 9/32; Chirag = 1/4 = 8/32.\nSo the new ratio = 15:9:8.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-040","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":40,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm's total assets are ₹9,00,000. This figure includes goodwill of ₹40,000. Its outside liabilities are creditors of ₹1,00,000 and a long-term loan of ₹60,000. The average profit is ₹96,000 and the normal rate of return is 12%. What is the value of goodwill by the capitalisation of average profits method?","image":"","options":["₹1,00,000","₹60,000","₹40,000","₹8,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Total assets = ₹9,00,000 (including goodwill ₹40,000); outside liabilities = 1,00,000 + 60,000 = ₹1,60,000.\nCapitalised value = 96,000 × 100/12 = ₹8,00,000.\nNet assets = (9,00,000 − 40,000) − 1,60,000 = ₹7,00,000. Goodwill is excluded, and both long-term and short-term liabilities are deducted.\nGoodwill = 8,00,000 − 7,00,000 = ₹1,00,000.\nWhy not ₹60,000: that answer forgets to exclude the existing goodwill from assets.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-041","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":41,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the adjustment of partners' capitals on admission are correct?\n(i) The total capital of the new firm can be found by dividing the new partner's capital by his share of profit.\n(ii) If an old partner's capital falls short and he brings in cash, the entry is Cash A/c Dr. To Partner's Capital A/c.\n(iii) If an old partner's capital is in excess and the excess is transferred to his current account, the entry is Partner's Current A/c Dr. To Partner's Capital A/c.\n(iv) Adjusting the surplus or deficiency through current accounts changes the cash balance of the firm.\n(v) When the total capital is specified, the new partner's capital is also calculated as his share of that total.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: dividing by the share means multiplying by its reciprocal, e.g. Rs. 20,000 ÷ $\\frac{1}{4}$ = Rs. 80,000.\n(ii) Correct: cash is received and the partner's capital is credited.\n(iii) Incorrect: for an excess, the capital account is debited and the current account is credited.\n(iv) Incorrect: a transfer to a current account is a book entry only and involves no cash.\n(v) Correct: as in NCERT Illustration 26, D brings 1/4 of Rs. 1,20,000.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-042","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":42,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A new partner brings his share of goodwill in cash, and it is deposited in the firm's bank account. Under the Premium for Goodwill method, which is the first journal entry?","image":"","options":["Premium for Goodwill A/c Dr. To Bank A/c","Bank A/c Dr. To Sacrificing Partners' Capital A/cs","Bank A/c Dr. To Premium for Goodwill A/c","New Partner's Current A/c Dr. To Premium for Goodwill A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When the premium is paid through the firm, the bank balance increases, so Bank A/c is debited.\nThe credit goes to Premium for Goodwill A/c, a temporary account.\nThat account is then closed by transferring it to the sacrificing partners' capital accounts.\nOption (2) skips the premium account, so it is not the first entry under this method.\nOption (4) applies only when the new partner does not bring goodwill in cash.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-043","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":43,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun, Bela and Chirag are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 4:3:1. From 1 April 2024 they decide to share future profits equally. On this date the firm's goodwill is valued at Rs. 72,000. Goodwill is not to appear in the books, and the adjustment is made through partners' capital accounts by a single journal entry.\nBy what amount will Arjun's capital account be credited in this adjustment entry?","image":"","options":["Rs. 36,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 3,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Old ratio is 4:3:1, i.e. $\\frac{12}{24}, \\frac{9}{24}, \\frac{3}{24}$. New ratio is equal, i.e. $\\frac{8}{24}$ each.\nSacrifice or gain = Old share − New share. Arjun sacrifices $\\frac{4}{24}$, Bela sacrifices $\\frac{1}{24}$ and Chirag gains $\\frac{5}{24}$.\nEntry: Chirag's Capital A/c Dr. Rs. 15,000 ($72,000 \\times \\frac{5}{24}$), To Arjun's Capital A/c and To Bela's Capital A/c.\nArjun's credit = $72,000 \\times \\frac{4}{24}$ = Rs. 12,000. Bela's credit = Rs. 3,000.\nRs. 36,000 is wrong because it is Arjun's old-ratio share of total goodwill, not his sacrifice.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-044","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":44,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the profits of a firm show a clearly rising or falling trend, goodwill is better valued on the basis of weighted average profits rather than simple average profits.\nReason (R): Under the weighted average method, the profits of the more recent years are given higher weights than those of earlier years.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true, but R is false","A is false, but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT says that when profits show an increasing or decreasing trend, it is advisable to use a weighted average.\nR is true. Weights such as 1, 2, 3, 4 are assigned so that the latest year carries the highest weight.\nR explains A. Giving recent years more weight makes the average reflect the current trend, which a simple average cannot do.\nNote: the weighted average is used only when it is specified.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-045","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":45,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"After admitting Zoya, the partners Xavier, Yash and Zoya will share profits in the ratio 3:2:1. They agree that the total capital of the firm will be Rs. 1,80,000 and that each partner's capital will be in the new profit sharing ratio. What is Yash's required capital?","image":"","options":["Rs. 60,000","Rs. 90,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 72,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Total capital = Rs. 1,80,000; new ratio = 3:2:1.\nFormula: Partner's capital = Total capital × his new share.\nSubstituting: Yash's share = $\\frac{2}{6}$, so Yash's capital = 1,80,000 × $\\frac{2}{6}$ = Rs. 60,000.\nRs. 90,000 is Xavier's capital ($\\frac{3}{6}$ share), not Yash's.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-046","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":46,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"The capital employed in a business is Rs. 4,00,000 and the normal rate of return is 10%. The average profit of the business is Rs. 55,000. What is the value of goodwill at 3 years' purchase of super profits?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,65,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 45,000","Rs. 1,20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Capital = Rs. 4,00,000; Normal rate = 10%; Average profit = Rs. 55,000; 3 years' purchase.\nFormula: Goodwill = (Average profit − Normal profit) × Years' purchase.\nSubstituting: Normal profit = 4,00,000 × 10/100 = Rs. 40,000. Super profit = 55,000 − 40,000 = Rs. 15,000. Goodwill = 15,000 × 3.\nSo goodwill = Rs. 45,000. Rs. 1,65,000 is wrong because it multiplies the average profit instead of the super profit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-047","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":47,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A and B share profits in the ratio 4:3. They admit C, and the new profit sharing ratio of A, B and C is 3:3:2. The firm's goodwill is valued at Rs. 1,12,000, and C brings his share of goodwill in cash. How much premium will be credited to A's capital account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 22,000","Rs. 16,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 14,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Old ratio A:B = 4:3; new ratio A:B:C = 3:3:2; goodwill = Rs. 1,12,000.\nA's sacrifice = 4/7 − 3/8 = 32/56 − 21/56 = 11/56. B's sacrifice = 3/7 − 3/8 = 24/56 − 21/56 = 3/56. So the sacrificing ratio is 11:3.\nC's premium = 2/8 × 1,12,000 = Rs. 28,000.\nA's share = 28,000 × 11/14 = Rs. 22,000.\nRs. 16,000 wrongly divides the premium in the old ratio 4:3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-048","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":48,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are factors affecting the value of goodwill as listed in NCERT?\nNature of business; Location; Efficiency of management; Market situation; Special advantages such as patents and import licences; Number of partners in the firm; Amount of drawings by partners","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"These five factors affect goodwill: nature of business, location, efficiency of management, market situation and special advantages.\nThe number of partners does not affect goodwill, because it has no bearing on the firm's ability to earn super profits.\nPartners' drawings also do not affect goodwill, because they are not a source of extra earning capacity.\nSo 5 of the 7 items are factors.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-049","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":49,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: A and B are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 3:2 with capitals of Rs. 60,000 and Rs. 40,000. They admit C for 1/5 share. On the date of admission:\n- The balance sheet shows a General Reserve of Rs. 15,000 and a debit balance in Profit and Loss Account of Rs. 5,000.\n- Revaluation results in a gain of Rs. 8,000.\n- C brings Rs. 10,000 as premium for goodwill, which is retained in the business. C acquires his share from A and B in the old ratio.\nWhat will be the balance of A's Capital Account after all adjustments?","image":"","options":["Rs. 76,800","Rs. 82,800","Rs. 70,800","Rs. 79,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Since C acquires his share in the old ratio, the sacrificing ratio is 3:2. Every adjustment below goes to A in the ratio 3/5.\nAdd general reserve: 15,000 × 3/5 = Rs. 9,000.\nLess P&L debit balance: 5,000 × 3/5 = Rs. 3,000.\nAdd revaluation gain: 8,000 × 3/5 = Rs. 4,800.\nAdd premium: 10,000 × 3/5 = Rs. 6,000.\nA's capital = 60,000 + 9,000 − 3,000 + 4,800 + 6,000 = Rs. 76,800.\nRs. 82,800 wrongly adds the P&L debit balance instead of deducting it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-050","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":50,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (Factor affecting goodwill) with List-II (Example):\nList-I\n(A) Efficiency of management\n(B) Market situation\n(C) Nature of business\n(D) Special advantages\nList-II\n(i) Firm makes high value-added products with stable demand\n(ii) Firm has a long-term contract for assured supply of raw materials\n(iii) Firm enjoys high productivity and cost efficiency\n(iv) Firm faces very limited competition","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Efficiency of management leads to high productivity and cost efficiency, so A matches (iii).\n(B) Market situation covers monopoly or limited competition, so B matches (iv).\n(C) Nature of business covers high value-added products or stable demand, so C matches (i).\n(D) Special advantages include long-term supply contracts, licences and patents, so D matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-051","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":51,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi and Kiran are partners. Mohan is admitted as a new partner, but he cannot bring his share of goodwill in cash. No goodwill account appears in the books of the firm. Which journal entry records the adjustment for goodwill?","image":"","options":["Goodwill A/c Dr. To All Partners' Capital A/cs (in new ratio)","Mohan's Current A/c Dr. To Ravi's and Kiran's Capital A/cs (in sacrificing ratio)","Mohan's Capital A/c Dr. To Premium for Goodwill A/c","Ravi's and Kiran's Capital A/cs Dr. To Mohan's Current A/c (in old ratio)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: according to NCERT, goodwill that the new partner does not bring in is debited to his current account.\nThe sacrificing partners are compensated by crediting their capital accounts in the sacrificing ratio.\nOption (1) raises goodwill and gives a share to the new partner as well. This is not allowed, because the old partners earned the goodwill before admission.\nOption (4) reverses the debit and the credit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-052","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":52,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation on admission of a partner) with List-II (accounting treatment):\nList-I\n(A) New partner pays premium for goodwill privately to the old partners\n(B) New partner brings premium for goodwill in cash through the firm\n(C) New partner does not bring his share of goodwill\n(D) Goodwill already appears in the old balance sheet\nList-II\n(i) Old partners' Capital A/cs Dr. (old ratio) To Goodwill A/c\n(ii) No entry in the books of the firm\n(iii) New Partner's Current A/c Dr. To Sacrificing Partners' Capital A/cs\n(iv) Bank A/c Dr. To Premium for Goodwill A/c","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(ii): a private payment does not pass through the firm, so no entry is made.\n(B)-(iv): cash received through the firm is credited to Premium for Goodwill A/c. It is later transferred to the sacrificing partners.\n(C)-(iii): goodwill not brought in is debited to the new partner's current account and credited to the sacrificing partners.\n(D)-(i): existing goodwill is written off against the old partners in their old ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-053","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":53,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding treatment of goodwill on admission of a partner is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Goodwill already appearing in the books is written off among old partners in the old profit sharing ratio.","If the new partner pays the premium privately to the old partners, no entry is passed in the firm's books.","As per AS 26, internally generated goodwill should not be recognised as an asset.","The share of goodwill not brought in cash by the new partner is credited to his current account."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: existing goodwill is written off by debiting old partners' capital accounts in the old ratio.\n(2) Correct: a private payment does not pass through the firm, so no entry is passed.\n(3) Correct: AS 26 does not allow self-generated goodwill to be recognised as an asset.\n(4) Incorrect: the new partner's current account is DEBITED for the goodwill he does not bring. The sacrificing partners' capital accounts are credited.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-054","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":54,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (item at the time of admission) with List-II (treatment):\nList-I\n(a) Advertisement suspense (deferred revenue expenditure) in the balance sheet\n(b) General Reserve\n(c) Creditor written off as he is not likely to claim\n(d) Unrecorded outstanding rent brought into books\nList-II\n(i) Credited to Revaluation Account\n(ii) Debited to old partners' capital accounts in old ratio\n(iii) Debited to Revaluation Account\n(iv) Credited to old partners' capital accounts in old ratio","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): Deferred revenue expenditure is an accumulated loss, written off to old partners in the old ratio.\n(b)-(iv): General reserve is an accumulated profit, credited to old partners in the old ratio.\n(c)-(i): A reduction in a liability is a gain, so it is credited to the Revaluation A/c.\n(d)-(iii): An unrecorded liability is a loss, so it is debited to the Revaluation A/c.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-055","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":55,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If goodwill appears in the books and the new partner does not bring his share of goodwill, the existing goodwill is first written off by debiting the old partners' capital accounts in their old profit sharing ratio.\nStatement II: In this case, the new partner's current account is debited with the full value of the firm's goodwill.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Goodwill appearing in the books is written off among the old partners in their old ratio.\nStatement II is incorrect. The new partner's current A/c is debited only with his share of goodwill, i.e. firm's goodwill × his profit share, not the firm's full goodwill.\nThe sacrificing partners are then credited in their sacrificing ratio.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-056","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":56,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Under the capitalisation of average profits method, the actual capital of the firm (net assets) is deducted from the capitalised value of average profits. Which of the following statements about calculating this capital is correct?","image":"","options":["It equals total assets, excluding goodwill and fictitious assets, minus outside liabilities, both long-term and short-term.","It equals total assets, including goodwill, minus only long-term outside liabilities.","It equals total assets, including fictitious assets, minus partners' capitals.","It equals total assets, excluding goodwill, minus only current liabilities."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT states that Firm's capital = Total assets (excluding goodwill and fictitious assets) − Outside liabilities. Outside liabilities include both long-term and short-term liabilities.\n(2) Wrong: goodwill must be excluded, and short-term liabilities must also be deducted.\n(3) Wrong: fictitious assets are excluded, and partners' capitals are not outside liabilities.\n(4) Wrong: long-term liabilities must also be deducted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-057","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":57,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Hari and Haqque are partners. Their partnership deed says nothing about admitting new partners. According to the Partnership Act 1932, when can they admit a new partner, Meena?","image":"","options":["Only when the partner with the larger profit share agrees","Only when all the existing partners agree unanimously","Only when a majority of the existing partners agree","Whenever the firm needs additional capital, without anyone's consent"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The Partnership Act 1932 says a new partner can be admitted only with the consent of all existing partners, unless the partnership deed provides otherwise.\nHere the deed is silent, so the default rule of unanimous consent applies.\nMajority consent (option 3) is the most tempting choice, but the Act requires every existing partner to agree.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-058","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":58,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: On the admission of a partner, a debit balance of Profit and Loss Account appearing in the balance sheet is debited to all partners' capital accounts, including the new partner, in the new profit sharing ratio.\nStatement II: Deferred revenue expenditure appearing in the balance sheet at the time of admission is written off by debiting the old partners' capital accounts in the old ratio.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. A debit balance of P&L is an accumulated loss of the old firm. It is borne only by the old partners in the old ratio, not by the new partner.\nStatement II is correct. Deferred revenue expenditure is also an accumulated loss. It is written off to the old partners' capital accounts in the old ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-059","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":59,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"L, M and N share profits in the ratio 4:3:1. They decide to share profits equally in future. On that date:\n- N's capital is Rs. 60,000.\n- The General Reserve is Rs. 48,000.\n- Revaluation shows a profit of Rs. 16,000.\n- Goodwill is valued at Rs. 72,000 and is not to appear in the books.\nWhat is N's capital after all adjustments?","image":"","options":["Rs. 53,000","Rs. 68,000","Rs. 83,000","Rs. 38,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: N's old share = $\\frac{1}{8}$ and new share = $\\frac{1}{3}$, so N gains $\\frac{8-3}{24} = \\frac{5}{24}$.\nReserve credited (old ratio): $48,000 \\times \\frac{1}{8}$ = Rs. 6,000. Revaluation profit credited: $16,000 \\times \\frac{1}{8}$ = Rs. 2,000.\nGoodwill debited: $72,000 \\times \\frac{5}{24}$ = Rs. 15,000.\nN's capital = 60,000 + 6,000 + 2,000 − 15,000 = Rs. 53,000.\nRs. 68,000 ignores the goodwill debit; Rs. 83,000 wrongly credits it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-060","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":60,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Amit and Bimal are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 3:2. They admit Charu for 1/4 share in future profits. On the date of admission, the balance sheet shows a General Reserve of Rs. 50,000. What amount of General Reserve will be credited to Amit's Capital Account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 30,000","Rs. 22,500","Rs. 25,000","Rs. 20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: General Reserve = Rs. 50,000. Old ratio of Amit and Bimal = 3:2.\nRule: Accumulated profits belong to the old partners. They are transferred to the old partners' capital accounts in the old ratio. The new partner gets no share.\nSubstituting: Amit's share = 50,000 × 3/5 = Rs. 30,000.\nRs. 22,500 is wrong because it uses Amit's new share (3/5 × 3/4), as if Charu were also entitled to the reserve.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-061","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":61,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A and B share profits in the ratio 3:2. C is admitted for a 1/4 share. He takes 1/6 from A and the rest of his share from B. What is the new profit sharing ratio of A, B and C?","image":"","options":["9 : 6 : 5","26 : 15 : 19","13 : 12 : 5","26 : 19 : 15"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: old shares A = 3/5 and B = 2/5; C's share = 1/4, of which 1/6 comes from A.\nB's sacrifice = 1/4 − 1/6 = 1/12.\nA's new share = 3/5 − 1/6 = 13/30 = 26/60.\nB's new share = 2/5 − 1/12 = 19/60.\nC's share = 1/4 = 15/60.\nSo the new ratio is 26 : 19 : 15.\n9 : 6 : 5 is wrong because it wrongly assumes C took his share in the old ratio.\nAnswer: (4)\n(Note: the option values below are the A : B ratio of each option.)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-062","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":62,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya and Lalit share profits in the ratio of 7:3. Manav is admitted for $\\frac{1}{5}$ share, which he acquires from them in their old ratio.\nAssertion (A): The new profit sharing ratio of Kavya, Lalit and Manav is 14:6:5.\nReason (R): When the new partner acquires his share in the old ratio, each old partner's new share equals his old ratio applied to the share remaining after the new partner's share.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Remaining share = $1 - \\frac{1}{5} = \\frac{4}{5}$.\nKavya = $\\frac{7}{10} \\times \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{28}{50}$ and Lalit = $\\frac{3}{10} \\times \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{12}{50}$.\nManav = $\\frac{10}{50}$, so the ratio is 28:12:10 = 14:6:5. A is true.\nR is true, and it is exactly the method used to obtain A, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-063","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":63,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"When the existing partners of a firm change their profit sharing ratio and goodwill is not to appear in the books, which journal entry adjusts goodwill?","image":"","options":["Gaining partner's Capital A/c Dr. To Sacrificing partner's Capital A/c","Sacrificing partner's Capital A/c Dr. To Gaining partner's Capital A/c","Goodwill A/c Dr. To All partners' Capital A/cs (in new ratio)","All partners' Capital A/cs Dr. (in old ratio) To Goodwill A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A partner who gains a larger share of future profits is in effect buying that share from the partner who gives it up.\nSo the gaining partner's capital is debited and the sacrificing partner's capital is credited with the proportionate value of goodwill.\nThe Dinesh–Ramesh–Suresh entry is an example: Suresh's Capital A/c Dr. To Dinesh's and Ramesh's Capital A/cs.\nOption (2) reverses the entry, so it would penalise the partner who gives up his share.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-064","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":64,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm closes its books on 31st March each year. On 1st October 2023, a machine costing Rs. 40,000 was purchased and wrongly debited to the Profit and Loss Account. The machine is to be depreciated at 10% p.a. on the reducing balance method. The reported profit for the year 2023-24 was Rs. 1,20,000. What is the adjusted profit for 2023-24 for valuing goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,58,000","Rs. 1,56,000","Rs. 1,60,000","Rs. 1,18,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: capital expenditure of Rs. 40,000 charged to revenue on 1 Oct 2023; depreciation 10% p.a.\nStep 1: Add back the capital expenditure: 1,20,000 + 40,000 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nStep 2: Deduct depreciation for 6 months only: 40,000 × 10/100 × 6/12 = Rs. 2,000.\nAdjusted profit = 1,60,000 − 2,000 = Rs. 1,58,000.\nRs. 1,56,000 wrongly charges a full year's depreciation.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-065","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":65,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Partners decide to change their profit sharing ratio. How many of the following items are adjusted in the partners' capital accounts in the OLD profit sharing ratio?\n(i) General Reserve\n(ii) Profit on revaluation of assets and reassessment of liabilities\n(iii) Debit balance of Profit and Loss Account\n(iv) Compensation for the firm's goodwill arising from the change\n(v) Goodwill already appearing in the books, written off","image":"","options":["Four","Three","Five","Two"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Old ratio: reserves are past profits.\n(ii) Old ratio: the revaluation gain relates to the period before the change.\n(iii) Old ratio: accumulated losses were also incurred in the past.\n(iv) Not old ratio: this is adjusted in the gaining/sacrificing ratio.\n(v) Old ratio: existing goodwill is written off among the partners who owned it.\nSo four items use the old ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-066","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":66,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"The goodwill of a firm is valued at Rs. 60,000. A new partner is admitted for 1/4 share in profits. He brings only his capital and nothing for goodwill. No goodwill account appears in the books. By what amount will the new partner's current account be debited?","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 45,000","Rs. 20,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: firm's goodwill = Rs. 60,000; new partner's share = 1/4.\nFormula: New partner's share of goodwill = Firm's goodwill × his share.\nSubstituting: 60,000 × 1/4 = Rs. 15,000.\nSo his current A/c is debited by Rs. 15,000, and the sacrificing partners' capital A/cs are credited.\nRs. 60,000 is the full goodwill of the firm, not his share.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-067","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":67,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm's average profits are Rs. 90,000. Its total assets are Rs. 10,00,000. This total includes goodwill of Rs. 40,000 and a fictitious asset (preliminary expenses) of Rs. 10,000. Outside liabilities are Rs. 2,50,000, and the normal rate of return is 10%.\nWhat is the value of goodwill by the capitalisation of super profits method?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,50,000","Rs. 1,60,000","Rs. 1,90,000","Rs. 2,00,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Average profits = Rs. 90,000; NRR = 10%\nCapital employed = Total assets − Goodwill − Fictitious assets − Outside liabilities\nCapital employed = 10,00,000 − 40,000 − 10,000 − 2,50,000 = Rs. 7,00,000\nNormal profit = 10% of 7,00,000 = Rs. 70,000\nSuper profit = 90,000 − 70,000 = Rs. 20,000\nGoodwill = 20,000 × $\\frac{100}{10}$ = Rs. 2,00,000\nThe distractors come from failing to exclude goodwill, the fictitious asset, or both.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-068","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":68,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are matters that require adjustment at the time of admission of a new partner?\n(i) New profit sharing ratio\n(ii) Valuation and adjustment of goodwill\n(iii) Revaluation of assets and reassessment of liabilities\n(iv) Distribution of accumulated profits (reserves)\n(v) Payment of all sundry creditors in full\n(vi) Adjustment of partners' capitals\n(vii) Sale of all fixed assets of the firm","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: the new profit sharing ratio must be worked out.\n(ii) Yes: goodwill must be valued and adjusted.\n(iii) Yes: assets are revalued and liabilities reassessed.\n(iv) Yes: reserves are distributed among the old partners.\n(v) No: creditors are not paid off, because the firm continues.\n(vi) Yes: capitals are adjusted if the partners agree.\n(vii) No: assets are not sold, since the firm is not dissolved.\nThat gives 5 matters.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-069","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":69,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"P and Q are partners with capitals of Rs. 70,000 and Rs. 50,000 after all adjustments. They admit R for 1/4 share in future profits, and R brings in Rs. 60,000 as capital. No value of goodwill is given. What is the hidden goodwill of the firm?","image":"","options":["Rs. 60,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R's capital = Rs. 60,000 for a 1/4 share; P = Rs. 70,000; Q = Rs. 50,000.\nImplied total capital = 60,000 × 4 = Rs. 2,40,000.\nActual combined capital = 70,000 + 50,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nGoodwill of the firm = 2,40,000 − 1,80,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nRs. 15,000 is only R's share (1/4 of 60,000), not the firm's goodwill.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-070","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":70,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation) with List-II (mode of reconstitution).\nList-I\n(A) Lata, Mira and Neel, sharing 3:2:1, agree to share profits equally because Neel brings in more capital.\n(B) Ojas leaves the firm due to old age, and the remaining partners continue the business.\n(C) Prem dies, and Rohit and Sana decide to carry on the business.\n(D) Tara and Uma take in Vivek with a 1/5 share in profits.\nList-II\n(1) Admission of a new partner\n(2) Retirement of a partner\n(3) Change in profit sharing ratio\n(4) Death of a partner","image":"","options":["A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1","A-1, B-2, C-4, D-3","A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1","A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The same partners change their ratio, so this is a change in profit sharing ratio (3).\n(B) Ojas withdraws from the business, so this is retirement (2).\n(C) The firm is reconstituted on Prem's death because the others continue the business (4).\n(D) Vivek enters the firm, so this is admission (1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-071","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":71,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"While valuing goodwill, it is found that the closing stock of 2022-23 was overvalued by Rs. 6,000. The reported profits were Rs. 50,000 for 2022-23 and Rs. 44,000 for 2023-24. What is the adjusted profit of 2023-24?","image":"","options":["Rs. 50,000","Rs. 38,000","Rs. 44,000","Rs. 56,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"An overvalued closing stock overstates that year's profit, so 2022-23 becomes 50,000 − 6,000 = Rs. 44,000.\nThe closing stock of 2022-23 is the opening stock of 2023-24.\nAn overvalued opening stock understates the next year's profit, so 6,000 must be added back.\nAdjusted profit of 2023-24 = 44,000 + 6,000 = Rs. 50,000.\nRs. 38,000 wrongly deducts the error again.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-072","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":72,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Goodwill exists only when a firm earns profits above the normal profits earned by similar firms in the industry.\nStatement II: A firm that has been incurring losses for years can still have goodwill if it is centrally located.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Goodwill is the present value of a firm's anticipated excess earnings, so it arises only when there are super profits.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT states that a firm earning only normal profits, or incurring losses, has no goodwill. Location raises goodwill only where super profits exist.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-073","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":73,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When partners change their profit sharing ratio, the General Reserve appearing in the balance sheet is credited to the partners' capital accounts in the old profit sharing ratio.\nReason (R): The reserve represents profits earned in the past, when the partners shared profits in the old ratio.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT requires accumulated profits and reserves to be transferred to capital accounts in the old ratio when the ratio changes.\nR is true. The reserve was built up from profits earned before the change, when the old ratio applied.\nR explains A because the old partners earned those profits under the old arrangement, so they must share them in that ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-074","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":74,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"The profits of a firm for the years ended 31st March were: 2022 Rs. 30,000; 2023 Rs. 36,000; 2024 Rs. 54,000. Goodwill is to be valued at 3 years' purchase of the weighted average profits, with weights 1, 2 and 3 respectively. What is the value of goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,32,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 44,000","Rs. 2,64,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: weights 1, 2 and 3; 3 years' purchase.\nProducts: 30,000 × 1 = 30,000; 36,000 × 2 = 72,000; 54,000 × 3 = 1,62,000. Total = Rs. 2,64,000.\nWeighted average profit = 2,64,000 ÷ 6 = Rs. 44,000.\nGoodwill = 44,000 × 3 = Rs. 1,32,000.\nRs. 1,20,000 wrongly uses the simple average of Rs. 40,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-075","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":75,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: P, Q and R share profits in the ratio 5:3:2. From April 1, 2024 they agree to share profits in the ratio 2:3:5. Goodwill is to be valued at 3 years' purchase of the average profits of the last three years: Rs. 40,000, Rs. 50,000 and Rs. 60,000. Goodwill will not appear in the books. What amount is credited to P's capital account for the goodwill adjustment?","image":"","options":["Rs. 45,000","Rs. 75,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 15,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Average profit = $\\frac{40,000+50,000+60,000}{3}$ = Rs. 50,000, so Goodwill = $50,000 \\times 3$ = Rs. 1,50,000.\nP: $\\frac{5}{10} \\to \\frac{2}{10}$, a sacrifice of $\\frac{3}{10}$. Q has no change. R: $\\frac{2}{10} \\to \\frac{5}{10}$, a gain of $\\frac{3}{10}$.\nP's credit = $1,50,000 \\times \\frac{3}{10}$ = Rs. 45,000.\nThe entry is R's Capital A/c Dr. To P's Capital A/c, Rs. 45,000. Rs. 75,000 would wrongly use P's old share of $\\frac{5}{10}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-076","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":76,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm has capital employed of Rs. 4,00,000. Its average profit is Rs. 60,000 and the normal rate of return in the industry is 12%. Calculate goodwill at 2½ years' purchase of super profits.","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,50,000","Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 24,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: capital Rs. 4,00,000; normal rate 12%; average profit Rs. 60,000; 2.5 years' purchase.\nFormula: Normal profit = Capital × Rate / 100.\nSubstituting: Normal profit = 4,00,000 × 12/100 = Rs. 48,000.\nSuper profit = 60,000 − 48,000 = Rs. 12,000.\nSo goodwill = 12,000 × 2.5 = Rs. 30,000.\nRs. 1,00,000 is wrong because it capitalises the super profit (12,000 × 100/12) instead of using years' purchase.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-077","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":77,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Before deciding the amount an old partner must bring in or withdraw for capital adjustment, his capital is first adjusted for goodwill, reserves and revaluation profit or loss.\nReason (R): Goodwill, reserves and revaluation profit or loss change the balances in the old partners' capital accounts.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the required capitals are compared with the old capitals after all adjustments for goodwill, reserves and revaluation have been made.\nR is true. Each of these items is credited or debited to the old partners' capital accounts, so the balances change.\nR explains A. Only the final balance gives the true surplus or shortage.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-078","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":78,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"When the value of goodwill is not given at the time of admission, it is inferred as hidden goodwill. Which of the following correctly gives the hidden goodwill of the firm?","image":"","options":["New partner's capital × reciprocal of his share, minus the total capitals of all partners (old partners after adjustments plus new partner)","New partner's capital × reciprocal of his share, minus the total capitals of the old partners only","New partner's capital × his share in profits","Total capitals of old partners minus the new partner's capital"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: The new partner's capital and share imply the firm's total capital, i.e. his capital × reciprocal of his share.\nStep 2: The actual total capital is the old partners' capitals (after adjustments) plus the new partner's capital.\nStep 3: Hidden goodwill = implied total capital − actual total capital.\nStep 4: Option (2) leaves out the new partner's own capital, so it overstates goodwill.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-079","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":79,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm has net assets of Rs. 5,00,000. It earns an average profit of Rs. 65,000, and the normal rate of return is 10%. Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(A) Normal profit\n(B) Super profit\n(C) Goodwill at 3 years' purchase of super profits\n(D) Goodwill by capitalisation of average profits\nList-II:\n(i) Rs. 1,50,000\n(ii) Rs. 50,000\n(iii) Rs. 15,000\n(iv) Rs. 45,000","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Normal profit = 5,00,000 × 10/100 = Rs. 50,000, so (ii).\n(B) Super profit = 65,000 − 50,000 = Rs. 15,000, so (iii).\n(C) Goodwill = 15,000 × 3 = Rs. 45,000, so (iv).\n(D) Capitalised value = 65,000 × 100/10 = 6,50,000. Goodwill = 6,50,000 − 5,00,000 = Rs. 1,50,000, so (i). This equals 15,000 × 100/10, as expected.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-080","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":80,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about the adjustment of capitals on the admission of a partner:\nStatement I: When the surplus or deficiency in old partners' capitals is adjusted through their current accounts, no cash is received or paid by the firm.\nStatement II: A partner whose adjusted capital is more than his required capital has his current account credited with the excess.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A transfer to a current account is only a book entry between two accounts of the same partner, so cash does not move.\nStatement II is correct. The entry for an excess is Partner's Capital A/c Dr. To Partner's Current A/c. The excess becomes an amount the firm owes the partner, so his current account is credited.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-081","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":81,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about admission of a partner.\nStatement I: The sacrifice made by an old partner is equal to his old share of profit minus his new share of profit.\nStatement II: The premium for goodwill brought in by the new partner is shared by the old partners in their new profit sharing ratio.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT states that Sacrifice = Old Share − New Share.\nStatement II is incorrect. The premium compensates the old partners for the share of profit they give up. It is therefore shared in the sacrificing ratio, not in the new ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-082","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":82,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a new partner acquires his entire share of profit from only one of the old partners, the shares of the other old partners remain unchanged.\nReason (R): The new share of an old partner is equal to his old share minus the share he surrenders in favour of the new partner.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: for each old partner, new share = old share − share surrendered.\nA is true: partners who surrender nothing keep their old share. For example, when the new partner takes his whole share from one partner, the other partner's new share stays equal to his old share.\nA follows directly from R, because the surrendered share is zero for those partners.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-083","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":83,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"On admission of a new partner, the partnership deed and the agreement are silent about how the new partner acquires his share from the existing partners. In which ratio is it assumed that the old partners surrender their shares?","image":"","options":["Equally","In their old profit sharing ratio","In the ratio of their capitals","In the new profit sharing ratio"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: if nothing is specified about how the new partner acquires his share, he is assumed to get it from the old partners in their old profit sharing ratio.\nWith this assumption, the sacrificing ratio equals the old ratio.\nOption (1), 'equally', applies only when it is expressly agreed.\nCapitals have no role in deciding the surrender, so option (3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-084","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":84,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements carefully.\nAssertion (A): A partnership firm whose average profit is only equal to the normal return on its capital employed has no goodwill.\nReason (R): Goodwill is the capitalised value of the differential profit capacity of a business, that is, of the profits it is expected to earn over and above the normal profits.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: Super profit = average profit − normal profit. If the firm earns only normal profit, its super profit is zero. NCERT says goodwill exists only when a firm earns super profits, so this firm has no goodwill.\nR is true: NCERT defines goodwill as the present value of a firm's anticipated excess earnings. It is the capitalised value of the differential profit capacity of the business.\nR explains A: goodwill depends on excess profits. With no excess profits, the capitalised value is nil.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-085","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":85,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following situations require valuation of goodwill in a partnership firm?\n(i) Change in profit sharing ratio among existing partners\n(ii) Admission of a new partner\n(iii) Retirement of a partner\n(iv) Death of a partner\n(v) Amalgamation of partnership firms\n(vi) A partner making drawings during the year\n(vii) Dissolution of the firm involving sale of business as a going concern","image":"","options":["4","5","6","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv) are occasions listed by NCERT. Each is a reconstitution in which some partner gains or loses a share in future profits.\n(v) Amalgamation of firms and (vii) sale of the business as a going concern are also listed occasions.\n(vi) is not an occasion: drawings do not change any partner's share in the firm's future profits.\nSo 6 situations need valuation of goodwill.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-086","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":86,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A and B share profits in the ratio 2:1. B's capital is Rs. 55,000. C is admitted for a 1/4 share, which he acquires from A and B in their old ratio.\nOn admission, the following adjustments are made:\n- General Reserve of Rs. 24,000 is distributed.\n- A revaluation loss of Rs. 3,000 is distributed.\n- C brings Rs. 12,000 as premium for goodwill.\n- C brings Rs. 50,000 as his capital.\nThe old partners' capitals are to be made proportionate to the new profit sharing ratio, using C's capital as the base. How much must B withdraw?","image":"","options":["Rs. 12,000","Rs. 17,000","Rs. 5,000","Rs. 16,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"New ratio = 2:1:1, and the sacrificing ratio = 2:1 (same as the old ratio).\nB's adjusted capital = 55,000 + 8,000 (1/3 of reserve) − 1,000 (1/3 of loss) + 4,000 (1/3 of premium) = Rs. 66,000.\nTotal capital = 50,000 × 4 = Rs. 2,00,000, so B's required capital = 2,00,000 × 1/4 = Rs. 50,000.\nSo B withdraws 66,000 − 50,000 = Rs. 16,000.\nRs. 17,000 ignores the revaluation loss.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-087","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":87,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about valuing goodwill by the average profits method are correct?\n(i) A loss incurred in any year is deducted while totalling the profits of the past years.\n(ii) If an annual charge for management cost is specified, it is deducted from the profit of each year before averaging.\n(iii) Weighted average profits should be used whenever profits fluctuate, even if not specified.\n(iv) Goodwill = Average profits × Number of years' purchase.\n(v) Capital expenditure wrongly charged to revenue is added back to profit with no adjustment for depreciation.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: losses reduce the total of past profits.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT deducts the specified management cost from every year's profit.\n(iii) Incorrect: weighted average is to be used only when it is specified.\n(iv) Correct: this is the basic formula of the method.\n(v) Incorrect: depreciation on the capitalised amount must also be deducted.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-088","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":88,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm's results for the last four years were: Year 1 profit Rs. 50,000; Year 2 profit Rs. 70,000; Year 3 loss Rs. 20,000; Year 4 profit Rs. 60,000. Calculate goodwill at 2 years' purchase of the average profits of these four years.","image":"","options":["Rs. 80,000","Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: profits of 50,000, 70,000 and 60,000 and a loss of 20,000; 2 years' purchase.\nThe loss is deducted while totalling: 50,000 + 70,000 − 20,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nAverage profits = 1,60,000 ÷ 4 = Rs. 40,000.\nGoodwill = 40,000 × 2 = Rs. 80,000.\nAdding the loss gives Rs. 1,00,000, and excluding the loss year gives Rs. 1,20,000. Both are wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-089","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":89,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"P and Q share profits in the ratio 5:3. They admit R for a $\\frac{1}{6}$ share. The new profit sharing ratio of P, Q and R is 3:2:1. The goodwill of the firm is valued at Rs. 1,20,000, and R brings his share of goodwill in cash. How much premium will be credited to Q's Capital A/c?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,000","Rs. 7,500","Rs. 8,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: old ratio 5:3, new ratio 3:2:1, goodwill Rs. 1,20,000.\nR's premium = $\\frac{1}{6}$ × 1,20,000 = Rs. 20,000.\nP's sacrifice = $\\frac{5}{8}-\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{3}{24}$. Q's sacrifice = $\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{24}$. So the sacrificing ratio is 3:1.\nQ's share of premium = 20,000 × $\\frac{1}{4}$ = Rs. 5,000.\nRs. 7,500 comes from wrongly using the old ratio. Rs. 8,000 comes from wrongly using the new ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-090","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":90,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meera and Kabir decide to admit Rohan into their firm. Goodwill is to be valued at 2 years' purchase of the simple average of the adjusted profits of the last three years. The reported profits were: 2021-22 Rs. 80,000; 2022-23 Rs. 1,00,000; 2023-24 Rs. 1,20,000. It is agreed that:\n(a) an annual charge of Rs. 10,000 for management cost be made for each year;\n(b) the closing stock of 2023-24 was overvalued by Rs. 6,000.\nWhat is the value of goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,76,000","Rs. 1,96,000","Rs. 1,80,000","Rs. 1,84,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Deduct Rs. 10,000 management cost from each year: 70,000; 90,000; 1,10,000.\nStep 2: The overvalued closing stock of 2023-24 overstates that year's profit: 1,10,000 − 6,000 = 1,04,000. No later year is affected within the period.\nStep 3: Total = 70,000 + 90,000 + 1,04,000 = 2,64,000. Average = Rs. 88,000.\nGoodwill = 88,000 × 2 = Rs. 1,76,000.\nIgnoring the stock error gives Rs. 1,80,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-091","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":91,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: A and B are partners sharing profits in the ratio 3:2. Their capitals are A Rs. 60,000 and B Rs. 40,000, and there is a General Reserve of Rs. 10,000. They admit C for $\\frac{1}{4}$ share, which C acquires equally from A and B. The following are agreed:\n(i) C brings Rs. 20,000 as premium for goodwill, which is retained in the business.\n(ii) Building is appreciated by Rs. 15,000.\n(iii) A provision for doubtful debts of Rs. 5,000 is created.\n(iv) An unrecorded liability of Rs. 2,000 is brought into the books.\nWhat will be the balance of A's Capital Account after these adjustments?","image":"","options":["Rs. 80,800","Rs. 82,800","Rs. 82,000","Rs. 74,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Revaluation: gain 15,000 − 5,000 − 2,000 = Rs. 8,000. A's share in the old ratio = $\\frac{3}{5}$ × 8,000 = Rs. 4,800.\nGeneral Reserve in the old ratio: A gets $\\frac{3}{5}$ × 10,000 = Rs. 6,000.\nPremium is shared in the sacrificing ratio, which is equal (1:1). So A gets Rs. 10,000, not Rs. 12,000 as the old ratio would give.\nA's capital = 60,000 + 4,800 + 6,000 + 10,000 = Rs. 80,800.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-092","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":92,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the same data, goodwill calculated by capitalising super profits is exactly equal to goodwill calculated by capitalising average profits.\nReason (R): Super profit × $\\frac{100}{\\text{Normal rate}}$ can be rewritten as Average profit × $\\frac{100}{\\text{Normal rate}}$ − Capital employed, because normal profit equals Capital × $\\frac{\\text{Normal rate}}{100}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT notes that both capitalisation methods give exactly the same goodwill.\nR is true. Super profit × 100/r = (Average profit − Capital × r/100) × 100/r = Average profit × 100/r − Capital.\nThe right-hand side is exactly 'capitalised value of average profits minus net assets'. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-093","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":93,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When partners change their profit sharing ratio, the gaining partner compensates the sacrificing partner for his share of goodwill.\nReason (R): A change in the profit sharing ratio among existing partners does not amount to reconstitution of the partnership firm.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: goodwill is adjusted by debiting the gaining partner's capital and crediting the sacrificing partner's capital.\nR is false: any change in the existing agreement, including a change in the profit sharing ratio, is a mode of reconstitution of the firm.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-094","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":94,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Xavier and Yamini share profits in the ratio of 4:3. They admit Zoya as a new partner. Xavier surrenders 1/4 of his share and Yamini surrenders 1/3 of her share in favour of Zoya. What is the new profit sharing ratio of Xavier, Yamini and Zoya?","image":"","options":["3:2:2","4:3:2","27:8:49","2:2:3"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Xavier's old share = 4/7 and Yamini's old share = 3/7.\nXavier's sacrifice = 1/4 of 4/7 = 1/7, so his new share = 4/7 − 1/7 = 3/7.\nYamini's sacrifice = 1/3 of 3/7 = 1/7, so her new share = 3/7 − 1/7 = 2/7.\nZoya's share = 1/7 + 1/7 = 2/7.\nSo the new ratio = 3:2:2. The ratio 27:8:49 comes from wrongly deducting 1/4 and 1/3 of the whole firm's profit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-095","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":95,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Asha and Bhuvan share profits in the ratio of 2:1. Chirag is admitted for $\\frac{1}{4}$ share. Match the mode of acquisition in List-I with the resulting new ratio (Asha : Bhuvan : Chirag) in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Acquired in the old ratio\n(b) Acquired equally from Asha and Bhuvan\n(c) Acquired wholly from Asha\n(d) $\\frac{1}{12}$ from Asha and $\\frac{1}{6}$ from Bhuvan\nList-II\n(i) 13 : 5 : 6\n(ii) 7 : 2 : 3\n(iii) 2 : 1 : 1\n(iv) 5 : 4 : 3","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Old shares are Asha $\\frac{2}{3}$ and Bhuvan $\\frac{1}{3}$.\n(a) Remaining $\\frac{3}{4}$ shared 2:1 gives $\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{1}{4}$ = 2:1:1, which is (iii).\n(b) Each gives $\\frac{1}{8}$: $\\frac{13}{24}, \\frac{5}{24}, \\frac{6}{24}$ = 13:5:6, which is (i).\n(c) Asha gives the whole share: $\\frac{5}{12}, \\frac{4}{12}, \\frac{3}{12}$ = 5:4:3, which is (iv).\n(d) $\\frac{7}{12}, \\frac{2}{12}, \\frac{3}{12}$ = 7:2:3, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-096","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":96,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When existing partners change their profit sharing ratio, the sacrificing ratio is always the same as their old profit sharing ratio.\nStatement II: A partner's gain on a change in the profit sharing ratio equals his new share minus his old share.","image":"","options":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both statements are true","Both statements are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. On a change of ratio, some partners gain and others sacrifice, so the sacrifice cannot simply follow the old ratio. For example, from 3:3:2 to equal, Dinesh and Ramesh sacrifice $\\frac{1}{24}$ each while Suresh gains $\\frac{2}{24}$.\nStatement II is true. Gain = New share − Old share, just as Sacrifice = Old share − New share.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-097","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":97,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Ritu and Sahil share profits in the ratio of 5:3. Tarun is admitted for $\\frac{3}{8}$ share. He takes $\\frac{1}{4}$ from Ritu and $\\frac{1}{8}$ from Sahil.\nStatement I: The new profit sharing ratio of Ritu, Sahil and Tarun is 3:2:3.\nStatement II: Sahil's new share of profit is $\\frac{3}{8}$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ritu's new share = $\\frac{5}{8} - \\frac{2}{8} = \\frac{3}{8}$.\nSahil's new share = $\\frac{3}{8} - \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{2}{8}$.\nTarun's share = $\\frac{3}{8}$.\nStatement I: True, because the new ratio is 3:2:3.\nStatement II: False, because Sahil's new share is $\\frac{2}{8} = \\frac{1}{4}$. $\\frac{3}{8}$ was his old share.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-098","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":98,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about valuation of goodwill:\nStatement I: Under the super profits method, goodwill is found by multiplying the super profits by the agreed number of years' purchase.\nStatement II: A firm that earns exactly the normal rate of return on its capital will still have some goodwill under the super profits method.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. Under the super profits method, Goodwill = Super profit × number of years' purchase.\nStatement II is false. If actual profit equals normal profit, the super profit is zero, so goodwill is zero.\nNCERT states that goodwill exists only when a firm earns super profits. A firm earning only normal profits, or incurring losses, has no goodwill.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-099","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":99,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A and B are partners. Their capitals after all adjustments are Rs. 70,000 and Rs. 50,000. C is admitted for 1/4 share in profits and brings Rs. 60,000 as capital. The value of goodwill is not given. Calculate C's share of hidden goodwill.","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 45,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: C's capital = Rs. 60,000 for 1/4 share.\nImplied total capital = 60,000 × 4 = Rs. 2,40,000.\nActual total capital = 70,000 + 50,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nHidden goodwill of the firm = 2,40,000 − 1,80,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nC's share = 60,000 × 1/4 = Rs. 15,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-100","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":100,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the question does not say how the new partner acquires his share from the old partners, the sacrificing ratio is taken to be the same as the old profit sharing ratio.\nReason (R): In the absence of any agreement, the new partner is assumed to acquire his share from the old partners in their old profit sharing ratio.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. NCERT says that if nothing is specified, the new partner is assumed to get his share from the old partners in their old ratio.\nA is true. Each old partner then gives up a share in proportion to his old share. As a result, the sacrificing ratio equals the old ratio.\nR is therefore the reason for A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-101","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":101,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the new profit sharing ratio on admission of a partner are correct?\n(i) The new partner acquires his share of profit from the old partners.\n(ii) If the new partner takes his share wholly from one old partner, the other old partner's share remains unchanged.\n(iii) If the new partner takes his share equally from the old partners, the old partners continue to share among themselves in their old ratio.\n(iv) If the new partner takes his share in the old ratio, the old partners continue to share among themselves in their old ratio.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the old partners sacrifice a part of their shares in favour of the new partner.\n(ii) Correct: only the surrendering partner's share falls.\n(iii) Incorrect: equal sacrifice changes the old partners' mutual ratio unless their old shares were equal. For example, 2:1 becomes 7:3.\n(iv) Correct: each old share is reduced proportionately, so their mutual ratio stays the same.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-102","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":102,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm has a capital employed of Rs. 4,00,000, and the normal rate of return in the industry is 12%. Its profits for the last three years were Rs. 60,000, Rs. 70,000 and Rs. 80,000. Goodwill is to be valued at 3 years' purchase of super profits. What is the value of goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 66,000","Rs. 2,10,000","Rs. 1,83,333","Rs. 1,44,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Capital = Rs. 4,00,000; normal rate of return = 12%; profits = 60,000, 70,000 and 80,000.\nFormula: Goodwill = (Average profit − Normal profit) × years' purchase.\nSubstituting: Average profit = 2,10,000 ÷ 3 = 70,000. Normal profit = 12% of 4,00,000 = 48,000. Super profit = 22,000.\nSo goodwill = 22,000 × 3 = Rs. 66,000.\nRs. 2,10,000 wrongly multiplies the average profit, not the super profit, by 3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-103","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":103,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Arun and Bina share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Chetan for 1/5 share. On the date of admission, the Balance Sheet shows:\nGeneral Reserve Rs. 30,000\nProfit and Loss A/c (debit balance) Rs. 10,000\nDeferred Revenue Expenditure Rs. 5,000\nWhat is the net amount credited to Arun's capital account on account of these items?","image":"","options":["Rs. 18,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 9,000","Rs. 6,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: reserve of Rs. 30,000 (a credit), P&L debit balance of Rs. 10,000 and deferred revenue expenditure of Rs. 5,000 (both accumulated losses). All three go to the old partners in the old ratio of 3:2.\nNet amount = 30,000 − 10,000 − 5,000 = Rs. 15,000.\nArun's share = 15,000 × 3/5 = Rs. 9,000.\nRs. 12,000 ignores the deferred revenue expenditure. Rs. 18,000 counts the reserve alone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-104","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":104,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the partnership deed is silent, a new partner can be admitted when partners holding a majority share in profits agree.\nStatement II: A new partner may bring his agreed capital either in cash or in kind, and the firm continues to exist after his admission.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Under the Partnership Act 1932, if the deed is silent, a new partner needs the consent of all existing partners. A majority, whether by number or by profit share, is not enough.\nStatement II is correct. NCERT says the new partner brings agreed capital in cash or in kind. Reconstitution ends the old agreement, but the firm itself continues.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-105","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":105,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"At the time of admission of a partner, it is found that an outstanding electricity bill of Rs. 2,000 has not been recorded in the books. Which journal entry correctly records it?","image":"","options":["Outstanding Electricity Bill A/c Dr. To Revaluation A/c","Revaluation A/c Dr. To Outstanding Electricity Bill A/c","Old Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. To Outstanding Electricity Bill A/c","Electricity Expenses A/c Dr. To Revaluation A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An unrecorded liability, once brought into the books, is a loss to the old partners.\nAll losses on revaluation are debited to the Revaluation Account, and the liability account is credited.\nSo the entry is: Revaluation A/c Dr. To Outstanding Electricity Bill A/c.\nOption (1) is the entry for a decrease in a liability, which is a gain, so it does not fit here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-106","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":106,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: The partners of Mehta Traders valued goodwill at ₹1,50,000 using the capitalisation of super profits method. The firm's capital employed is ₹6,00,000 and the normal rate of return is 10%.\nA new partner now insists that goodwill be valued at 4 years' purchase of super profits instead. What will be the value of goodwill under this new basis?","image":"","options":["₹60,000","₹3,00,000","₹1,50,000","₹2,40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Goodwill (capitalisation of super profits) = ₹1,50,000; rate = 10%; capital = ₹6,00,000.\nFind super profit: Goodwill = Super profit × 100/10, so Super profit = 1,50,000 × 10/100 = ₹15,000.\nCheck: Normal profit = 6,00,000 × 10% = ₹60,000, so average profit = 60,000 + 15,000 = ₹75,000.\nNew basis: Goodwill = 15,000 × 4 = ₹60,000.\nWhy not ₹3,00,000: that answer multiplies the average profit of ₹75,000 by 4 instead of the super profit.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-107","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":107,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A business earns an average profit of Rs. 72,000. The normal rate of return in similar businesses is 12%. Its total assets (excluding goodwill) are Rs. 6,20,000 and its outside liabilities are Rs. 1,00,000. What is the goodwill by the capitalisation of average profits method?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,20,000","Rs. 80,000","Rs. 6,00,000","Rs. 8,640"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Average profit = Rs. 72,000; Normal rate = 12%; Assets = Rs. 6,20,000; Outside liabilities = Rs. 1,00,000.\nFormula: Goodwill = (Average profit × 100 / Normal rate) − Net assets.\nSubstituting: Capitalised value = 72,000 × 100/12 = Rs. 6,00,000. Net assets = 6,20,000 − 1,00,000 = Rs. 5,20,000.\nSo goodwill = 6,00,000 − 5,20,000 = Rs. 80,000. Rs. 6,00,000 is only the capitalised value.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-108","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":108,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Gita and Rita share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Sita, and the new profit sharing ratio of Gita, Rita and Sita is 2:2:1. Sita brings Rs. 15,000 as premium for goodwill in cash. What amount of this premium is credited to Rita's Capital A/c?","image":"","options":["Nil","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 7,500","Rs. 9,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Old shares are Gita $\\frac{3}{5}$ and Rita $\\frac{2}{5}$. New shares are Gita $\\frac{2}{5}$ and Rita $\\frac{2}{5}$.\nSacrifice = Old share − New share.\nGita's sacrifice = $\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{1}{5}$. Rita's sacrifice = $\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0$.\nOnly Gita sacrifices, so the whole Rs. 15,000 goes to Gita.\nRita gets nil. Rs. 6,000 is what you get by wrongly using the old ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-109","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":109,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Ishaan and Kabir are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 5:3. They admit Lata as a new partner. The new profit sharing ratio of Ishaan, Kabir and Lata is agreed at 3:2:1. What is the sacrificing ratio of Ishaan and Kabir?","image":"","options":["3:1","5:3","1:3","3:2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: old shares are Ishaan $\\frac{5}{8}$ and Kabir $\\frac{3}{8}$. New shares are Ishaan $\\frac{3}{6}$ and Kabir $\\frac{2}{6}$.\nFormula: Sacrifice = Old share − New share.\nIshaan: $\\frac{5}{8}-\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{15-12}{24}=\\frac{3}{24}$.\nKabir: $\\frac{3}{8}-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{9-8}{24}=\\frac{1}{24}$.\nSo the sacrificing ratio is 3:1. The old ratio 5:3 does not apply because the new ratio is given.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-110","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":110,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm has capital employed of Rs. 2,00,000. Its average profit over the last few years is Rs. 30,000, and the normal rate of return in similar businesses is 12%. What is the firm's super profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 24,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 3,600","Rs. 54,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Capital = Rs. 2,00,000; Normal rate = 12%; Average profit = Rs. 30,000.\nFormula: Normal profit = Capital × Normal rate / 100, and Super profit = Average profit − Normal profit.\nSubstituting: Normal profit = 2,00,000 × 12/100 = Rs. 24,000. Super profit = 30,000 − 24,000.\nSo super profit = Rs. 6,000. Rs. 24,000 is only the normal profit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-111","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":111,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"X and Y are partners with capitals of Rs. 70,000 and Rs. 50,000 respectively. They admit Z for 1/4 share in profits, and Z brings Rs. 60,000 as capital. The value of goodwill is not given. What is the hidden goodwill of the firm?","image":"","options":["Rs. 60,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 40,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Z brings Rs. 60,000 for a 1/4 share.\nImplied total capital of the new firm = 60,000 × 4 = Rs. 2,40,000.\nActual combined capital = 70,000 + 50,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nSo goodwill of the firm = 2,40,000 − 1,80,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nRs. 15,000 is only Z's 1/4 share of this goodwill, not the firm's goodwill.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-112","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":112,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a new partner does not bring his share of goodwill, a goodwill account for the full value of the firm's self-generated goodwill should not be shown as an asset in the Balance Sheet.\nReason (R): According to AS 26, internally generated goodwill should not be recognised as an asset.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Self-generated goodwill is not shown as an asset. Instead, the goodwill is adjusted by debiting the new partner's current A/c and crediting the sacrificing partners.\nR is true. AS 26 states that internally generated goodwill should not be recognised as an asset.\nR explains A, because the bar on showing such goodwill comes directly from AS 26.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-113","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":113,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Under AS 26, internally generated (self-generated) goodwill should not be recognised as an asset in the books.\nStatement II: Goodwill found by capitalising super profits is always different from goodwill found by capitalising average profits for the same data.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. AS 26 says internally generated goodwill must not be recognised as an asset. If such goodwill is ever raised, it must be written off.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT shows that both capitalisation methods give exactly the same goodwill.\nCheck with NCERT's example (net assets Rs. 8,20,000, average profit Rs. 1,00,000, normal rate 10%):\nCapitalised value of average profits = Rs. 10,00,000, so goodwill = 10,00,000 − 8,20,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nSuper profit = 1,00,000 − 82,000 = Rs. 18,000, so goodwill = 18,000 × 100/10 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-114","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":114,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi and Sunil admit Tarun as a partner. On the date of admission, the following were agreed:\n(i) Stock of Rs. 40,000 is to be reduced by 10%.\n(ii) Building is to be appreciated by Rs. 20,000.\n(iii) A provision for doubtful debts of 5% is to be created on debtors of Rs. 60,000 (no provision existed earlier).\n(iv) Creditors of Rs. 2,000 were unrecorded.\nWhat is the net gain or loss on revaluation?","image":"","options":["Gain of Rs. 11,000","Gain of Rs. 15,000","Gain of Rs. 9,000","Gain of Rs. 13,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Debit side (losses): stock 10% of 40,000 = Rs. 4,000; provision 5% of 60,000 = Rs. 3,000; unrecorded creditors = Rs. 2,000. Total = Rs. 9,000.\nCredit side (gains): building = Rs. 20,000.\nNet = 20,000 − 9,000 = Rs. 11,000. The credit balance is a gain.\nRs. 13,000 is wrong because it omits the unrecorded creditors.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-115","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":115,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Amar and Bela share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Chetan for 1/4 share, and the new profit sharing ratio of Amar, Bela and Chetan is 2:1:1. The firm's goodwill is valued at Rs. 80,000. Chetan brings only Rs. 8,000 in cash towards his share of goodwill. The unpaid balance is debited to his current account. No goodwill appears in the books.\nWhat total amount will be credited to Bela's Capital A/c for goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 8,000","Rs. 7,200","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 4,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Chetan's share of goodwill = 1/4 × 80,000 = Rs. 20,000. This is Rs. 8,000 in cash plus Rs. 12,000 debited to his current account.\nSacrifice: Amar = 3/5 − 2/4 = 2/20; Bela = 2/5 − 1/4 = 3/20. So the sacrificing ratio is 2:3.\nThe full Rs. 20,000 (cash portion and current account portion together) goes to the sacrificing partners: Bela = 3/5 × 20,000 = Rs. 12,000.\nRs. 8,000 wrongly uses the old ratio. Rs. 7,200 wrongly shares only the unpaid Rs. 12,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-116","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":116,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"X and Y share profits in the ratio of 2:1. They admit Z for 1/4 share. Debtors stand at Rs. 50,000, and the existing provision for doubtful debts is Rs. 1,500. On Z's admission it is agreed that:\n(i) The provision is to be maintained at 4% of debtors.\n(ii) Stock of Rs. 30,000 is to be revalued at Rs. 33,000.\n(iii) Machinery of Rs. 40,000 is to be depreciated by 10%.\nWhat is X's share of the result of revaluation?","image":"","options":["Loss of Rs. 1,000","Loss of Rs. 500","Loss of Rs. 1,500","Loss of Rs. 2,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Provision required = 4% of 50,000 = Rs. 2,000. The existing provision is Rs. 1,500, so the additional charge is Rs. 500 (debit).\nStock gain = 33,000 − 30,000 = Rs. 3,000 (credit).\nMachinery loss = 10% of 40,000 = Rs. 4,000 (debit).\nNet = 3,000 − 500 − 4,000 = loss of Rs. 1,500.\nThe loss is shared by the old partners in the old ratio 2:1, so X's share = 1,500 × 2/3 = Rs. 1,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-117","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":117,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Read the following statements about the Average Profits Method of valuing goodwill.\nStatement I: The method rests on the assumption that a newly set up business will not be able to earn profits during its first few years.\nStatement II: Under this method, goodwill is calculated on the excess of average profits over the normal return on capital employed.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect and Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A buyer of a running business pays, as goodwill, the profits he expects to earn in the first few years, because a new business would not earn them.\nStatement II is incorrect. Valuing goodwill on profits in excess of the normal return is the Super Profits Method. The Average Profits Method multiplies the actual average profits by the years' purchase.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-118","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":118,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): On the admission of a new partner, the balance in the Profit and Loss Account (credit) is transferred to the old partners' capital accounts in their old profit sharing ratio.\nReason (R): The new partner is not entitled to any share in profits that the firm earned before his admission.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Accumulated profits, such as a credit balance in the P&L Account, are distributed among the old partners in the old ratio.\nR is true. These profits were earned when only the old partners shared in the business, so the incoming partner has no claim on them.\nR states exactly why A happens, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-119","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":119,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"The profits of a partnership firm for the last three years were Rs. 2,40,000, Rs. 2,80,000 and Rs. 3,20,000. Goodwill is to be valued at $2\\frac{1}{2}$ years' purchase of the average profits of the last three years. What is the value of goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 7,00,000","Rs. 8,40,000","Rs. 7,20,000","Rs. 5,60,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: profits Rs. 2,40,000, Rs. 2,80,000 and Rs. 3,20,000; 2.5 years' purchase.\nFormula: Goodwill = Average profits × Number of years' purchase.\nAverage profits = 8,40,000 ÷ 3 = Rs. 2,80,000.\nGoodwill = 2,80,000 × 2.5 = Rs. 7,00,000.\nRs. 8,40,000 uses 3 years' purchase, and Rs. 5,60,000 uses 2 years' purchase.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-120","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":120,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Pooja and Qadir share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit Rohan for a 1/5 share in profits, which he acquires from them in their old ratio. Rohan brings Rs. 50,000 as capital and Rs. 10,000 as premium for goodwill in cash, both paid through the firm. What amount of premium will be credited to Pooja's capital account?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 4,000","Rs. 30,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: premium = Rs. 10,000. Rohan acquires his share in the old ratio 3:2, so the sacrificing ratio is also 3:2.\nRule: premium is shared by the old partners in their sacrificing ratio.\nPooja's share = 10,000 × 3/5 = Rs. 6,000. Qadir gets Rs. 4,000.\nThe Rs. 50,000 is Rohan's own capital and is not shared.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-121","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":121,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A and B share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit C for 1/5 share, which he acquires from A and B in their old ratio. C brings Rs. 50,000 as capital, and the capitals of A and B are to be made proportionate to the new profit sharing ratio on the basis of C's capital. After all adjustments, A's capital is Rs. 1,30,000 and B's is Rs. 60,000. How much cash must B bring in?","image":"","options":["Rs. 20,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 40,000","Nil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"New ratio: A = 3/5 × 4/5 = 12/25; B = 2/5 × 4/5 = 8/25; C = 5/25.\nTotal capital, based on C's capital = 50,000 × 5 = Rs. 2,50,000.\nB's required capital = 8/25 × 2,50,000 = Rs. 80,000.\nB must bring in 80,000 − 60,000 = Rs. 20,000.\nA's required capital is 12/25 × 2,50,000 = Rs. 1,20,000, so A withdraws Rs. 10,000.\nRs. 40,000 wrongly uses B's old share 2/5 of the total capital instead of the new share.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-122","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":122,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Asha, Bimal and Charu are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 4:3:2. On 1 April they admit Dev as a new partner. To give Dev his share, Asha surrenders 1/4 of her share, Bimal surrenders 1/3 of his share and Charu surrenders 1/6 of her share. What is the new profit sharing ratio of Asha, Bimal, Charu and Dev?","image":"","options":["9:6:5:7","9:6:4:8","6:4:3:5","12:9:6:7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Old shares: Asha 4/9, Bimal 3/9, Charu 2/9.\nAsha's sacrifice = 1/4 × 4/9 = 1/9, so her new share = 3/9 = 9/27.\nBimal's sacrifice = 1/3 × 3/9 = 1/9, so his new share = 2/9 = 6/27.\nCharu's sacrifice = 1/6 × 2/9 = 1/27, so her new share = 6/27 − 1/27 = 5/27.\nDev = 1/9 + 1/9 + 1/27 = 7/27. Check: 9 + 6 + 5 + 7 = 27.\nSo the new ratio = 9:6:5:7.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-123","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":123,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the profits of a firm show a steadily rising trend, the profits of recent years are given higher weights while valuing goodwill by the weighted average profits method.\nReason (R): When profits show a clear trend, recent profits are a better indicator of the profits likely to be earned in the future.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT advises giving higher weightage, such as 1, 2, 3, 4, to recent years when profits show an increasing or decreasing trend.\nR is true. Goodwill reflects expected future profits, and when there is a trend, recent years reflect future earning capacity better than older years.\nR therefore explains why recent years get higher weights.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-124","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":124,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (event on change in profit sharing ratio) with List-II (journal entry):\nList-I\n(a) Increase in value of building\n(b) Unrecorded liability brought into books\n(c) Transfer of General Reserve\n(d) Loss on revaluation\nList-II\n(i) Revaluation A/c Dr. To Liability A/c\n(ii) Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. (old ratio) To Revaluation A/c\n(iii) Building A/c Dr. To Revaluation A/c\n(iv) General Reserve A/c Dr. To Partners' Capital A/cs (old ratio)","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(iii): An increase in an asset is a gain, so the asset is debited and Revaluation A/c is credited.\n(b)-(i): An unrecorded liability is a loss, so Revaluation A/c is debited.\n(c)-(iv): General Reserve is debited and credited to partners' capitals in the old ratio.\n(d)-(ii): A revaluation loss is debited to partners' capitals in the old ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-125","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":125,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: M and N share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit O for a $\\frac{1}{4}$ share, which O acquires equally from M and N. The firm's goodwill is valued at Rs. 80,000, and Goodwill A/c already appears in the books at Rs. 30,000. O brings Rs. 50,000 as capital and his share of goodwill in cash. The existing goodwill is to be written off, and M and N withdraw half of the premium credited to them. What is the net decrease in M's Capital A/c as a result of these goodwill entries?","image":"","options":["Rs. 13,000","Rs. 8,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Premium = $\\frac{1}{4}$ × 80,000 = Rs. 20,000. The sacrifice is equal, so M is credited with Rs. 10,000.\nExisting goodwill is written off in the old ratio 3:2, so M is debited with 30,000 × $\\frac{3}{5}$ = Rs. 18,000.\nM withdraws half of his premium, so he is debited with Rs. 5,000.\nNet effect = 10,000 − 18,000 − 5,000 = −Rs. 13,000, a decrease of Rs. 13,000.\nRs. 8,000 ignores the withdrawal. Rs. 12,000 shares the premium in the old ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-126","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":126,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are factors that affect the value of a firm's goodwill?\n(i) Business located at a place with heavy customer traffic\n(ii) Limited competition in the market\n(iii) Assured supply of electricity at a low rate\n(iv) Number of partners in the firm\n(v) Efficient management leading to cost efficiency\n(vi) Profit sharing ratio among the partners","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Factor: location.\n(ii) Factor: market situation.\n(iii) Factor: special advantage.\n(v) Factor: efficiency of management.\n(iv) and (vi) are not factors: the number of partners and the way they divide profits do not change the firm's capacity to earn super profits.\nSo 4 of the items are factors.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-127","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":127,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Patel Traders has capital employed of Rs. 3,00,000. It earns an average profit of Rs. 57,000, and the normal rate of return in the industry is 15%. What is the goodwill of the firm by capitalising its super profits?","image":"","options":["Rs. 36,000","Rs. 3,80,000","Rs. 80,000","Rs. 1,800"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Capital = Rs. 3,00,000; Average profit = Rs. 57,000; Normal rate = 15%.\nFormula: Goodwill = Super profit × 100 / Normal rate.\nSubstituting: Normal profit = 3,00,000 × 15/100 = Rs. 45,000. Super profit = 57,000 − 45,000 = Rs. 12,000. Goodwill = 12,000 × 100/15.\nSo goodwill = Rs. 80,000. Rs. 3,80,000 is the capitalised value of average profits, not the goodwill.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-128","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":128,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Past profits are being adjusted before calculating goodwill by the average profits method. How many of the following items would be ADDED to the reported profit of the year concerned?\n(i) Capital expenditure wrongly charged to revenue in that year\n(ii) Depreciation on the capital expenditure in (i)\n(iii) Overvaluation of closing stock of that year\n(iv) Undervaluation of closing stock of that year\n(v) Annual management cost not charged to profits\n(vi) Overvaluation of opening stock of that year","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Added: the profit was understated because a capital item was expensed.\n(ii) Deducted: depreciation on the capitalised amount was never provided.\n(iii) Deducted: an overstated closing stock inflates the profit.\n(iv) Added: an understated closing stock depresses the profit.\n(v) Deducted: the management charge reduces the profit.\n(vi) Added: an overstated opening stock understates the profit.\nThe items added are (i), (iv) and (vi), which makes 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-129","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":129,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When Kiran is admitted as a partner into the firm of Anil and Bala, the partnership firm is reconstituted.\nReason (R): Any change in the existing agreement among partners ends that agreement, and a new agreement comes into being.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Admitting a new partner is one of the modes of reconstitution.\nR is true. NCERT says any change in the existing agreement ends that agreement and brings a new one into being, although the firm itself continues.\nAdmitting Kiran changes the agreement, so R correctly explains why the firm is reconstituted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-130","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":130,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Meera's Bakery ran for years in a narrow lane with few customers. The partners shifted it to a shop beside a busy metro station that sees heavy foot traffic all day. Sales and profits rose sharply, and the value of the firm's goodwill increased.\nWhich factor affecting goodwill is mainly illustrated here?","image":"","options":["Efficiency of management","Location","Special advantages","Nature of business"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT lists location as a factor affecting goodwill. A business that is centrally located, or placed where customer traffic is heavy, tends to have higher goodwill.\nIn this case the products and the management did not change; only the place of business changed, to a high-footfall spot.\nEfficiency of management would show up as better productivity or cost control, which the case does not mention.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-131","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":131,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm has net assets (total assets excluding goodwill minus outside liabilities) of Rs. 5,00,000. Its average profit is Rs. 72,000, and the normal rate of return in similar businesses is 12%.\nStatement I: Goodwill calculated by capitalising the average profits is Rs. 1,00,000.\nStatement II: Goodwill at 3 years' purchase of super profits is Rs. 36,000.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Capitalised value of average profits = 72,000 × 100/12 = Rs. 6,00,000.\nGoodwill = 6,00,000 − 5,00,000 (net assets) = Rs. 1,00,000. So Statement I is correct.\nStatement II: Normal profit = 12% of 5,00,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nSuper profit = 72,000 − 60,000 = Rs. 12,000.\nGoodwill = 12,000 × 3 = Rs. 36,000. So Statement II is correct.\nNote: capitalising the super profit (12,000 × 100/12) also gives Rs. 1,00,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-132","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":132,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya and Lalit share profits in the ratio of 2:1. Manav is admitted for 1/4 share. Match the manner of acquisition in List-I with the new ratio of Kavya, Lalit and Manav in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) In the old ratio\n(B) Equally from Kavya and Lalit\n(C) Wholly from Kavya\n(D) Wholly from Lalit\nList-II\n(I) 5:4:3\n(II) 8:1:3\n(III) 2:1:1\n(IV) 13:5:6","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Remaining 3/4 is split 2:1, giving Kavya 1/2, Lalit 1/4 and Manav 1/4. Ratio 2:1:1, so A-III.\n(B) Each surrenders 1/8: Kavya 2/3 − 1/8 = 13/24, Lalit 1/3 − 1/8 = 5/24, Manav 6/24. Ratio 13:5:6, so B-IV.\n(C) Kavya 2/3 − 1/4 = 5/12, Lalit 4/12, Manav 3/12. Ratio 5:4:3, so C-I.\n(D) Kavya 8/12, Lalit 4/12 − 3/12 = 1/12, Manav 3/12. Ratio 8:1:3, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-133","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":133,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When a new partner is admitted and only his share of profit is given, the sacrificing ratio of the old partners is taken to be the same as their old profit sharing ratio.\nReason (R): If nothing is specified, the new partner is assumed to acquire his share from the old partners in their old profit sharing ratio.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true, but R is false.","A is false, but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that the sacrificing ratio is usually the same as the old ratio when no other ratio is specified.\nR is true. If nothing is said about how the new partner gets his share, it is assumed that he acquires it from the old partners in their old ratio.\nR explains A. Because each old partner gives up the new partner's share in proportion to his old share, the sacrifices fall in the old ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-134","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":134,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"X and Y share profits in the ratio 3:2. After all adjustments, their capitals are Rs. 70,000 and Rs. 50,000 respectively. Z is admitted for $\\frac{1}{4}$ share in profits and brings Rs. 60,000 as capital. No value of goodwill is given, and Z does not bring any amount for goodwill. What amount will be debited to Z's Current Account for his share of the firm's hidden goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 15,000","Rs. 60,000","Rs. 20,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Z's capital is Rs. 60,000 for a $\\frac{1}{4}$ share.\nImplied total capital of the firm = 60,000 × 4 = Rs. 2,40,000.\nActual total capital = 70,000 + 50,000 + 60,000 = Rs. 1,80,000.\nHidden goodwill of the firm = 2,40,000 − 1,80,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nZ's share = 60,000 × $\\frac{1}{4}$ = Rs. 15,000. This is debited to Z's Current A/c and credited to X and Y in their sacrificing ratio (3:2).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-135","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":135,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): On the admission of a new partner, a loss on revaluation of assets and reassessment of liabilities is debited to the capital accounts of the old partners in their old profit sharing ratio.\nReason (R): The changes in the values of assets and liabilities relate to the period before the new partner's admission.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT says the balance of the Revaluation Account, whether gain or loss, is transferred to the old partners' capital accounts in the old ratio.\nR is true: the rise or fall in asset and liability values took place while only the old partners owned the firm.\nR explains A: because the change belongs to the pre-admission period, only the old partners bear it, in the ratio they shared profits then.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-136","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":136,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (journal entry) with List-II (situation during capital adjustment):\nList-I\n(a) Cash A/c Dr. To B's Capital A/c\n(b) A's Capital A/c Dr. To Cash A/c\n(c) A's Capital A/c Dr. To A's Current A/c\n(d) B's Current A/c Dr. To B's Capital A/c\nList-II\n(i) Excess capital transferred to current account\n(ii) Deficiency in capital made good by bringing cash\n(iii) Deficiency in capital adjusted through current account\n(iv) Excess capital withdrawn in cash","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): Cash comes in and B's capital rises, so B makes good a deficiency.\n(b)-(iv): A's capital falls and cash goes out, so A withdraws an excess.\n(c)-(i): A's capital is reduced and his current account is credited, so an excess is transferred.\n(d)-(iii): B's current account is debited to raise his capital, so a deficiency is adjusted without cash.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-137","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":137,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about goodwill and its valuation is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Goodwill may be described as the present value of a firm's anticipated excess earnings.","Because goodwill is intangible, a single universally accepted method gives its exact value, so partners need not agree on a method.","Goodwill found by capitalising average profits equals goodwill found by capitalising super profits, using the same data.","Goodwill places its buyer in a position to earn profits above those of other firms in the same industry."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: this is one of NCERT's definitions of goodwill.\n(2) Incorrect: because goodwill is intangible, it is very difficult to value accurately. Different methods can give different values, so the method should be agreed between the existing partners and the incoming partner.\n(3) Correct: NCERT notes that the two capitalisation approaches give exactly the same goodwill.\n(4) Correct: paying for goodwill means paying for the ability to earn super profits.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-138","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":138,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Ravi, Tara and Uma share profits in the ratio 5:4:3. They decide to share future profits equally. Which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["Ravi sacrifices, Tara neither gains nor sacrifices, and Uma gains","Ravi and Tara both sacrifice, and Uma gains","Ravi sacrifices, while Tara and Uma both gain","Uma sacrifices, and Ravi gains"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write all shares in twelfths. Old shares are $\\frac{5}{12}, \\frac{4}{12}, \\frac{3}{12}$; new equal shares are $\\frac{4}{12}$ each.\nRavi: $\\frac{5}{12} \\to \\frac{4}{12}$, so he sacrifices $\\frac{1}{12}$.\nTara: $\\frac{4}{12} \\to \\frac{4}{12}$, so there is no change.\nUma: $\\frac{3}{12} \\to \\frac{4}{12}$, so she gains $\\frac{1}{12}$.\nOption (2) is tempting, but Tara's share does not fall.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-139","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":139,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Meena and Naveen share profits in the ratio of 2:1. Om is admitted for $\\frac{1}{6}$ share, which he acquires equally from Meena and Naveen. What is Naveen's new share of profit?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{5}{18}$","$\\frac{1}{3}$","$\\frac{1}{6}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Naveen's old share = $\\frac{1}{3}$. Om's share = $\\frac{1}{6}$, taken equally from both partners.\nEach partner surrenders $\\frac{1}{6} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{12}$.\nNaveen's new share = $\\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{12} = \\frac{4}{12} - \\frac{1}{12} = \\frac{3}{12} = \\frac{1}{4}$.\nThe value $\\frac{5}{18}$ results from wrongly assuming the old ratio. The full new ratio is 7:3:2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-140","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":140,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Mehta and Nair share profits in the ratio 7:5. They admit Ojha as a new partner. The new profit sharing ratio of Mehta, Nair and Ojha is 3:2:1. What is the sacrificing ratio of Mehta and Nair?","image":"","options":["7:5","1:1","3:2","5:7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: old shares are $\\frac{7}{12}$ and $\\frac{5}{12}$. New shares are $\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{6}{12}$ and $\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{4}{12}$.\nMehta's sacrifice = $\\frac{7}{12}-\\frac{6}{12}=\\frac{1}{12}$.\nNair's sacrifice = $\\frac{5}{12}-\\frac{4}{12}=\\frac{1}{12}$.\nSo the sacrificing ratio = 1:1.\nThe old ratio 7:5 does not apply here, because the new ratio is given.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-141","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":141,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"At the time of admission of a new partner, an investment worth Rs. 8,000 that was not recorded in the books is to be brought into account. Which journal entry is correct?","image":"","options":["Revaluation A/c Dr. 8,000\n To Investment A/c 8,000","Investment A/c Dr. 8,000\n To Revaluation A/c 8,000","Investment A/c Dr. 8,000\n To Old Partners' Capital A/cs 8,000","Investment A/c Dr. 8,000\n To New Partner's Capital A/c 8,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An unrecorded asset is a gain to the firm, so the Revaluation Account is credited and the Asset Account is debited.\nThe entry is: Investment A/c Dr., To Revaluation A/c.\nOption (1) is the entry for an unrecorded liability or a fall in an asset's value, so it is wrong here.\nOptions (3) and (4) are wrong because individual items are first passed through the Revaluation Account. Only its net balance goes to the old partners.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-142","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":142,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"P and Q are partners sharing profits in the ratio 3:1. They admit R, and the new profit sharing ratio of P, Q and R is agreed at 2:2:1. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["P and Q sacrifice in their old ratio 3:1","P sacrifices $\\frac{7}{20}$ and Q gains $\\frac{3}{20}$; the entire sacrifice is made by P","Q sacrifices $\\frac{3}{20}$ and P sacrifices $\\frac{4}{20}$ in favour of R","P sacrifices $\\frac{4}{20}$ and Q neither gains nor sacrifices"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Formula: Sacrifice = Old share − New share.\nP: old $\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{15}{20}$, new $\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{8}{20}$. P sacrifices $\\frac{7}{20}$.\nQ: old $\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{20}$, new $\\frac{8}{20}$. Q gains $\\frac{3}{20}$.\nR's share is $\\frac{4}{20}$. Check: $\\frac{7}{20}=\\frac{3}{20}+\\frac{4}{20}$, so P alone sacrifices, in favour of both Q and R.\nOption (1) wrongly assumes the old ratio applies. Option (3) treats Q as a sacrificing partner, but Q actually gains.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-143","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":143,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"In accounting, goodwill of a partnership firm is best classified as which type of asset?","image":"","options":["A fictitious asset","A current asset","An intangible asset","A tangible fixed asset"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Goodwill is the money value of a firm's good name, reputation and wide business connections.\nThese advantages help the firm earn more profit, but they cannot be seen or touched, so goodwill is an intangible asset.\nIt is not fictitious, because it has real value: it reflects the firm's expected excess earnings. A fictitious asset, by contrast, is an expense not yet written off.\nIt is not a current asset, because it is not meant to be turned into cash in the normal operating cycle.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-144","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":144,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A firm that earns only normal profits, or is incurring losses, has no goodwill.\nReason (R): Goodwill is the capitalised value attached to the differential (excess) profit-earning capacity of a business.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that goodwill exists only when a firm earns super profits. A firm earning only normal profits, or making losses, has no goodwill.\nR is true: goodwill is defined as the capitalised value of the differential profit capacity, that is, the present value of anticipated excess earnings.\nR explains A: if there are no profits above the normal level, there is nothing to capitalise, so goodwill is zero.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-145","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":145,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm earns average profits of Rs. 72,000. The normal rate of return in similar businesses is 12%. The firm's net assets (total assets excluding goodwill minus outside liabilities) are Rs. 5,00,000. What is the value of goodwill by the capitalisation of average profits method?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 6,00,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 36,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Average profit = Rs. 72,000; normal rate of return = 12%; net assets = Rs. 5,00,000.\nFormula: Capitalised value = Average profit × 100 / normal rate of return; Goodwill = Capitalised value − Net assets.\nSubstituting: Capitalised value = 72,000 × 100/12 = Rs. 6,00,000. Goodwill = 6,00,000 − 5,00,000.\nSo goodwill = Rs. 1,00,000.\nRs. 12,000 is only the super profit (72,000 − 60,000); it still has to be capitalised.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-146","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":146,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Arjun and Bela share profits in the ratio of 4:3. They admit Chetan. Arjun surrenders $\\frac{1}{4}$ of his share and Bela surrenders $\\frac{1}{3}$ of her share in favour of Chetan. What is Chetan's share in profits?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{2}{7}$","$\\frac{7}{12}$","$\\frac{1}{7}$","$\\frac{3}{7}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Arjun's old share = $\\frac{4}{7}$ and Bela's old share = $\\frac{3}{7}$.\nArjun's sacrifice = $\\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{4}{7} = \\frac{1}{7}$.\nBela's sacrifice = $\\frac{1}{3} \\times \\frac{3}{7} = \\frac{1}{7}$.\nChetan's share = $\\frac{1}{7} + \\frac{1}{7} = \\frac{2}{7}$, and the new ratio is 3:2:2.\nOption (2) wrongly adds the fractions $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$ themselves, instead of applying them to each partner's share.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-147","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":147,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Case: Anita and Bhavesh share profits in the ratio 2:1, with capitals of Rs. 60,000 and Rs. 40,000. The Balance Sheet also shows a General Reserve of Rs. 15,000, Debtors of Rs. 40,000 with a Provision for doubtful debts of Rs. 1,000, Stock of Rs. 30,000 and a Building of Rs. 80,000. Charu is admitted for 1/4 share, and the new ratio is 2:1:1. The agreed terms are:\n• Building appreciated by 15%\n• Stock reduced by 10%\n• Provision for doubtful debts to be maintained at 5% of debtors\n• An unrecorded liability of Rs. 2,000 to be recorded\n• An unrecorded investment of Rs. 3,000 to be brought into books\nWhat will Anita's capital be after these adjustments (ignore goodwill)?","image":"","options":["Rs. 72,000","Rs. 74,000","Rs. 76,000","Rs. 66,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Gains: building 15% × 80,000 = 12,000; investment 3,000. Total gains = 15,000.\nLosses: stock 10% × 30,000 = 3,000; provision raised from 1,000 to 2,000, a loss of 1,000; liability 2,000. Total losses = 6,000.\nRevaluation gain = 15,000 − 6,000 = 9,000. Anita's share in the old ratio = 9,000 × 2/3 = 6,000.\nReserve share = 15,000 × 2/3 = 10,000.\nAnita's capital = 60,000 + 6,000 + 10,000 = Rs. 76,000.\nRs. 72,000 wrongly uses the new ratio (1/2) instead of the old ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-148","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":148,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (situation on admission) with List-II (journal entry):\nList-I\n(a) New partner's share of goodwill not brought in cash\n(b) Goodwill already appearing in the books\n(c) Premium for goodwill brought in cash through the firm\n(d) Old partners withdraw the premium credited to them\nList-II\n(i) Old Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. To Goodwill A/c (old ratio)\n(ii) New Partner's Current A/c Dr. To Sacrificing Partners' Capital A/cs\n(iii) Bank A/c Dr. To Premium for Goodwill A/c\n(iv) Old Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. To Bank A/c","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a)-(ii): Goodwill not brought in cash is debited to the new partner's current A/c and credited to the sacrificing partners.\n(b)-(i): Existing goodwill is written off against the old partners' capitals in their old ratio.\n(c)-(iii): Cash brought in as premium is recorded through the Bank A/c and the Premium for Goodwill A/c.\n(d)-(iv): A withdrawal reduces the old partners' capitals and the bank balance.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-149","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":149,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Read the two statements about the alternative way of recording premium brought in cash by a new partner.\nStatement I: The cash brought for goodwill may first be credited to the new partner's Capital A/c. It is then transferred by debiting his Capital A/c and crediting the sacrificing partners' Capital A/cs.\nStatement II: Under this alternative route, the new partner's Capital A/c finally shows his capital plus the premium for goodwill.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT allows Bank A/c Dr. To New Partner's Capital A/c, followed by New Partner's Capital A/c Dr. To Sacrificing Partners' Capital A/cs.\nStatement II is incorrect. The second entry removes the premium from the new partner's account.\nHis capital therefore shows only the capital he brought, exactly as under the Premium for Goodwill method.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-150","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":150,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Alok, Bharat and Chirag share profits in the ratio 2:2:1. Dev is admitted for $\\frac{1}{5}$ share, which he takes wholly from Alok. The total capital of the new firm is fixed at Rs. 2,00,000, and each partner's capital is to be in the new ratio. After all adjustments, the capitals are: Alok Rs. 30,000, Bharat Rs. 90,000 and Chirag Rs. 45,000. Dev brings his capital in cash, any deficiency is brought in cash, and any excess is withdrawn in cash. What is the net increase in the firm's cash because of these capital adjustments?","image":"","options":["Rs. 35,000","Rs. 50,000","Rs. 40,000","Rs. 25,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"New shares: Alok = $\\frac{2}{5}$ − $\\frac{1}{5}$ = $\\frac{1}{5}$; Bharat = $\\frac{2}{5}$; Chirag = $\\frac{1}{5}$; Dev = $\\frac{1}{5}$. The ratio is 1:2:1:1.\nRequired capitals: Alok Rs. 40,000; Bharat Rs. 80,000; Chirag Rs. 40,000; Dev Rs. 40,000.\nCash in: Alok 10,000 + Dev 40,000 = Rs. 50,000.\nCash out: Bharat 10,000 + Chirag 5,000 = Rs. 15,000.\nNet increase in cash = 50,000 − 15,000 = Rs. 35,000.\nRs. 50,000 is wrong because it ignores the withdrawals.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-151","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":151,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Pooja and Qadir are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 5:3. They admit Rohan for $\\frac{1}{4}$ share in future profits. Nothing is agreed about how Rohan acquires his share from the old partners. What is the new profit sharing ratio of Pooja, Qadir and Rohan?","image":"","options":["15 : 9 : 8","5 : 3 : 1","15 : 9 : 6","9 : 15 : 8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Since nothing is specified, assume Rohan acquires his share from Pooja and Qadir in their old ratio of 5:3.\nRemaining share = $1 - \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}$.\nPooja = $\\frac{5}{8} \\times \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{15}{32}$ and Qadir = $\\frac{3}{8} \\times \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{9}{32}$.\nRohan = $\\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{8}{32}$, so the new ratio is 15:9:8.\nOption (2) simply adds a 1 for Rohan. Option (3) wrongly takes Rohan's share as $\\frac{6}{32}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-152","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":152,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Pooja and Qadir share profits in the ratio of 2:1. They admit Rehan for 1/6 share in profits, which he acquires equally from Pooja and Qadir. Calculate the new profit sharing ratio of Pooja, Qadir and Rehan.","image":"","options":["7:3:2","10:5:3","2:1:1","9:3:2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Old ratio 2:1. Rehan's share = 1/6, taken equally, so each old partner surrenders 1/12.\nPooja = 2/3 − 1/12 = 8/12 − 1/12 = 7/12.\nQadir = 1/3 − 1/12 = 4/12 − 1/12 = 3/12.\nRehan = 1/6 = 2/12.\nSo the new ratio = 7:3:2. The ratio 10:5:3 wrongly assumes the share was taken in the old ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-153","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":153,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Harsh and Ishaan share profits in the ratio 3:2. Jatin is admitted and brings Rs. 10,000 as premium for goodwill in cash. The premium is credited to the old partners in their sacrificing ratio, which is the same as the old ratio. The old partners then withdraw 40% of the premium credited to them. What amount is debited to Harsh's Capital A/c for this withdrawal?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,600","Rs. 2,400","Rs. 4,000","Rs. 6,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Premium = Rs. 10,000; sacrificing ratio = 3:2; withdrawal = 40%.\nHarsh's share of premium = $10,000 \\times \\frac{3}{5}$ = Rs. 6,000.\nWithdrawal entry: Harsh's Capital A/c Dr. To Bank A/c.\nAmount withdrawn by Harsh = 40% of 6,000 = Rs. 2,400.\nSo Harsh's capital is debited with Rs. 2,400. Rs. 1,600 is Ishaan's withdrawal.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-154","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":154,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Kavya and Leena are partners. Mohit is admitted and brings Rs. 12,000 as premium for goodwill in cash. The firm follows the alternative method, so the whole cash is first credited to Mohit's Capital A/c. Which journal entry gives the premium to the old partners?","image":"","options":["Mohit's Capital A/c Dr. To Kavya's and Leena's Capital A/cs (in sacrificing ratio)","Kavya's and Leena's Capital A/cs Dr. To Mohit's Capital A/c (in sacrificing ratio)","Mohit's Capital A/c Dr. To Premium for Goodwill A/c","Goodwill A/c Dr. To Mohit's Capital A/c"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Under the alternative method, Bank A/c is debited and the New Partner's Capital A/c is credited with both the capital and the premium.\nThe premium does not belong to Mohit. It compensates the old partners for the share of profit they gave up.\nSo Mohit's Capital A/c is debited and the sacrificing partners' Capital A/cs are credited in the sacrificing ratio.\nOption (2) reverses the entry. That would wrongly increase Mohit's capital.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-155","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":155,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding goodwill and AS 26 is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Internally generated goodwill should not be recognised as an asset.","Purchased goodwill may be shown as an asset, but it should be written off within a period not exceeding 10 years.","Goodwill already appearing in the books at the time of admission is written off among all partners, including the new partner, in the new ratio.","Internally generated brands and publishing titles should not be recognised as intangible assets."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Option (1) is correct: AS 26 does not allow internally generated goodwill to be recognised.\nOption (2) is correct: purchased goodwill may be recorded, but it must be written off within 10 years.\nOption (3) is incorrect: existing goodwill belongs to the old partners. It is written off only in their capital accounts, in the old ratio, not in the new ratio.\nOption (4) is correct, as stated in AS 26.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-156","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":156,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A firm whose average profit is exactly equal to its normal profit has no goodwill under the super profits method.\nReason (R): Goodwill exists only when a firm earns profits in excess of the normal profits on its capital employed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. When average profit equals normal profit, super profit = 0. Goodwill = 0 × years' purchase = 0.\nR is true. NCERT says goodwill exists only when the firm earns super profits. A firm earning only normal profits, or incurring losses, has no goodwill.\nR explains why A holds: there is no excess earning to pay for.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-157","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":157,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm's profits for the last three years were Rs. 2,40,000, Rs. 2,70,000 and Rs. 3,00,000. What is the firm's goodwill at 3 years' purchase of the average profits?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,40,000","Rs. 8,10,000","Rs. 9,00,000","Rs. 2,70,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: profits of Rs. 2,40,000, Rs. 2,70,000 and Rs. 3,00,000; 3 years' purchase.\nFormula: Goodwill = Average profit × Number of years' purchase.\nSubstituting: Average profit = 8,10,000 / 3 = Rs. 2,70,000.\nGoodwill = 2,70,000 × 3 = Rs. 8,10,000.\nRs. 9,00,000 is the wrong choice because it multiplies only the latest year's profit by 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-158","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":158,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"X and Y are partners sharing profits in the ratio 2:1. They admit Z for 1/4 share in profits, which Z acquires from X and Y in their old ratio. Z brings Rs. 40,000 as capital. After all adjustments for goodwill, reserves and revaluation, the capitals of X and Y are Rs. 90,000 and Rs. 25,000. The capitals of X and Y are to be made proportionate to the new profit sharing ratio, taking Z's capital as the base. Any deficiency is brought in cash and any excess is withdrawn in cash. How much cash will Y bring in?","image":"","options":["Rs. 5,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 15,000","Rs. 25,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Z's share is 1/4 and his capital is Rs. 40,000. The remaining 3/4 is shared by X and Y in 2:1.\nNew shares: X = 2/4, Y = 1/4, Z = 1/4. The new ratio is 2:1:1.\nTotal capital of the new firm = 40,000 × 4/1 = Rs. 1,60,000.\nY's required capital = 1,60,000 × 1/4 = Rs. 40,000.\nY's existing capital is Rs. 25,000, so Y brings 40,000 − 25,000 = Rs. 15,000.\nFor comparison, X's required capital is Rs. 80,000, so X withdraws Rs. 10,000.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-159","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":159,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (revaluation item at the time of admission) with List-II (journal entry):\nList-I\n(A) Decrease in the value of stock\n(B) A creditor not likely to claim is written off\n(C) An unrecorded investment is brought into the books\n(D) Provision made for an outstanding electricity bill\nList-II\n(i) Revaluation A/c Dr. To Liability A/c\n(ii) Asset A/c Dr. To Revaluation A/c\n(iii) Revaluation A/c Dr. To Asset A/c\n(iv) Liability A/c Dr. To Revaluation A/c","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A fall in an asset's value is a loss, so Revaluation is debited and the asset credited: (iii).\n(B) A reduction in a liability is a gain, so the liability is debited and Revaluation credited: (iv).\n(C) An unrecorded asset is a gain, so the asset is debited and Revaluation credited: (ii).\n(D) An unrecorded or increased liability is a loss, so Revaluation is debited and the liability credited: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-160","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":160,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"P and Q share profits in the ratio 3:2. They admit R for 1/5 share. The firm's goodwill is valued at Rs. 1,50,000. R brings Rs. 50,000 as capital and only Rs. 12,000 towards his share of goodwill. No goodwill account exists in the books. By what amount will R's current account be debited?","image":"","options":["Rs. 18,000","Rs. 30,000","Rs. 12,000","Rs. 42,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: goodwill = Rs. 1,50,000; R's share = 1/5; cash brought for goodwill = Rs. 12,000.\nR's share of goodwill = 1,50,000 × 1/5 = Rs. 30,000.\nAmount not brought = 30,000 − 12,000 = Rs. 18,000.\nEntry: Premium for Goodwill A/c Dr. 12,000 and R's Current A/c Dr. 18,000, To P's and Q's Capital A/cs 30,000.\nSo R's current A/c is debited by Rs. 18,000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-161","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":161,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Meena and Naveen are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 3:2. They admit Om, and the new profit sharing ratio of Meena, Naveen and Om is 2:3:1. The goodwill of the firm is valued at Rs. 60,000. Om brings his share of goodwill in cash. Naveen's gain is adjusted through the partners' capital accounts. What is the total amount credited to Meena's capital account for goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 16,000","Rs. 10,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 36,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: old shares are Meena $\\frac{18}{30}$ and Naveen $\\frac{12}{30}$. New shares are $\\frac{10}{30}$, $\\frac{15}{30}$ and $\\frac{5}{30}$.\nMeena's sacrifice = $\\frac{8}{30}$. Naveen's gain = $\\frac{3}{30}$. Om's gain = $\\frac{5}{30}$. The whole sacrifice is by Meena.\nOm's premium = $60{,}000\\times\\frac{5}{30}$ = Rs. 10,000, all credited to Meena.\nNaveen's capital is debited $60{,}000\\times\\frac{3}{30}$ = Rs. 6,000, also credited to Meena.\nSo Meena's total credit = $60{,}000\\times\\frac{8}{30}$ = Rs. 16,000.\nRs. 10,000 is wrong because it ignores Naveen's gain.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-162","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":162,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm earns super profits of Rs. 12,000 per year. The normal rate of return in similar businesses is 12%. If goodwill is taken as the capitalised value of super profits, what is the value of goodwill?","image":"","options":["Rs. 1,44,000","Rs. 1,00,000","Rs. 1,20,000","Rs. 10,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: Super profits = Rs. 12,000; Normal rate of return (NRR) = 12%\nFormula: Goodwill = Super profits × $\\frac{100}{NRR}$\nSubstituting: Goodwill = 12,000 × $\\frac{100}{12}$\nSo goodwill = Rs. 1,00,000\nRs. 1,44,000 is a common error: it multiplies by 12 instead of dividing by the rate.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-163","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":163,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"The goodwill of a firm, calculated by capitalising its super profits at a normal rate of return of 10%, is Rs. 60,000. The net assets (capital employed) of the firm are Rs. 4,00,000. What is the firm's average profit?","image":"","options":["Rs. 40,000","Rs. 6,000","Rs. 34,000","Rs. 46,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Goodwill = Rs. 60,000; Normal rate = 10%; Net assets = Rs. 4,00,000.\nStep 1: Super profit = Goodwill × 10/100 = Rs. 6,000.\nStep 2: Normal profit = 4,00,000 × 10/100 = Rs. 40,000.\nStep 3: Average profit = Normal profit + Super profit = 40,000 + 6,000 = Rs. 46,000.\nCheck: Capitalised value = 46,000 × 100/10 = 4,60,000, and 4,60,000 − 4,00,000 = Rs. 60,000.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-164","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":164,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Meera and Naveen are partners sharing profits in the ratio of 5:3. Om is admitted for 1/4 share, which he takes as 3/16 from Meera and 1/16 from Naveen.\nStatement I: The new profit sharing ratio of Meera, Naveen and Om is 7:5:4.\nStatement II: Since Om gets 1/4 share, Meera's new share is 3/4 of her old share.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Meera = 10/16 − 3/16 = 7/16; Naveen = 6/16 − 1/16 = 5/16; Om = 4/16. The ratio is 7:5:4. True.\nStatement II: 3/4 of Meera's old share = 3/4 × 5/8 = 15/32. Her actual new share is 7/16 = 14/32. False. Her share is reduced by the specified 3/16, not proportionately, because Om did not acquire his share in the old ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-165","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":165,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"The profits of a firm for four years are given below. The profits are given weights of 1, 2, 3 and 4 respectively, with the latest year weighted highest.\nYear 1: Rs. 40,000\nYear 2: Rs. 50,000\nYear 3: Rs. 30,000\nYear 4: Rs. 60,000\nWhat is the goodwill at 2 years' purchase of the weighted average profits?","image":"","options":["Rs. 94,000","Rs. 90,000","Rs. 86,000","Rs. 1,41,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Four years' profits with weights 1, 2, 3 and 4. Goodwill = 2 years' purchase.\nProducts: 40,000 × 1 = 40,000; 50,000 × 2 = 1,00,000; 30,000 × 3 = 90,000; 60,000 × 4 = 2,40,000.\nTotal of products = Rs. 4,70,000. Total of weights = 10.\nWeighted average profit = 4,70,000 ÷ 10 = Rs. 47,000.\nGoodwill = 47,000 × 2 = Rs. 94,000.\nRs. 90,000 is wrong because it uses the simple average (Rs. 45,000). Rs. 86,000 is wrong because it reverses the weights.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-166","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":166,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"An established firm earns more than the normal rate of return on its capital. When it admits a new partner, he must contribute an amount over and above his capital. What is this amount called, and why is it paid?","image":"","options":["Premium for goodwill, to compensate the sacrificing partners for losing part of their share in super profits","Revaluation profit, to make up for the increase in the value of assets","General reserve, to build up the firm's accumulated profits","Interest on capital, to compensate the old partners for the capital they invested"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The new partner takes his profit share from the old partners. They therefore lose part of their share in the firm's super profits.\nTo compensate them, he brings an extra amount called premium for goodwill.\nRevaluation profit comes from changes in the values of assets and liabilities, not from a payment by the new partner.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-167","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":167,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (transaction on admission) with List-II (journal entry).\nList-I:\n(A) Premium brought in cash through the firm\n(B) Premium distributed among sacrificing partners\n(C) Old partners withdraw their share of premium\n(D) Goodwill already in the books is written off\nList-II:\n(i) Old Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. To Goodwill A/c\n(ii) Bank A/c Dr. To Premium for Goodwill A/c\n(iii) Premium for Goodwill A/c Dr. To Sacrificing Partners' Capital A/cs\n(iv) Old Partners' Capital A/cs Dr. To Bank A/c","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv","A-iii, B-ii, C-iv, D-i","A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(ii): Cash received is debited to Bank and credited to Premium for Goodwill.\n(B)-(iii): The premium account is closed by crediting the sacrificing partners.\n(C)-(iv): A withdrawal reduces capital and bank, so capital is debited and Bank credited.\n(D)-(i): Existing goodwill is written off against old partners' capitals, with Goodwill A/c credited.\nNote that (C) and (D) both debit capital; they differ in the account credited.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-168","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":168,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A, B and C share profits in the ratio 4:3:2. D is admitted for 1/5 share. A surrenders 1/4 of his share and B surrenders 1/6 of his share in favour of D, and the balance of D's share comes from C. What is C's new share of profit?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{17}{90}$","$\\frac{2}{9}$","$\\frac{1}{6}$","$\\frac{7}{45}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Old shares are A = 4/9, B = 3/9 and C = 2/9. D's share = 1/5.\nA's sacrifice = 1/4 × 4/9 = 1/9. B's sacrifice = 1/6 × 3/9 = 1/18.\nC's sacrifice = 1/5 − 1/9 − 1/18 = 1/5 − 1/6 = 1/30.\nC's new share = 2/9 − 1/30 = 20/90 − 3/90 = 17/90.\nThe full new ratio is 30:25:17:18.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-169","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":169,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following situations require valuation of goodwill in a partnership firm, as per NCERT?\n(i) Change in profit sharing ratio among existing partners\n(ii) Admission of a new partner\n(iii) Sale of an old machine by the firm\n(iv) Death of a partner\n(v) Amalgamation of partnership firms\n(vi) Dissolution involving sale of the business as a going concern","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Yes: the gaining partner compensates the sacrificing partner.\n(ii) Yes: the new partner pays for his share of goodwill.\n(iii) No: selling a single asset does not change anyone's right to future super profits.\n(iv) Yes: the deceased partner's share of goodwill must be settled.\n(v) Yes and (vi) Yes: both appear in NCERT's list.\nSo 5 of the 6 situations need goodwill valuation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-170","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":170,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"A firm has capital employed of ₹4,00,000. Its average profit is ₹60,000, and the normal rate of return in similar businesses is 12%. What is the value of goodwill at 3 years' purchase of super profits?","image":"","options":["₹36,000","₹1,80,000","₹1,00,000","₹12,000"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: Capital = ₹4,00,000; normal rate = 12%; average profit = ₹60,000; 3 years' purchase.\nFormula: Normal profit = Capital × Rate/100. Super profit = Average profit − Normal profit. Goodwill = Super profit × years' purchase.\nSubstituting: Normal profit = 4,00,000 × 12/100 = ₹48,000. Super profit = 60,000 − 48,000 = ₹12,000. Goodwill = 12,000 × 3 = ₹36,000.\nWhy not ₹1,80,000: that figure multiplies the whole average profit by 3, which is the average profits method, not the super profits method.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"CBSE12-ACC-ADMISSION-171","exam":"CBSE","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Admission of a Partner","qno":171,"subject":"Accountancy","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Admission of a Partner","type":"mcq","question":"Kabir and Leena are partners. Meera is admitted for $\\frac{1}{4}$ share and pays her premium for goodwill of Rs. 20,000 directly to Kabir and Leena in their personal capacity, outside the business. Which journal entry is passed in the books of the firm for this premium?","image":"","options":["Bank A/c Dr. To Premium for Goodwill A/c","No entry is passed in the books of the firm","Meera's Capital A/c Dr. To Kabir's and Leena's Capital A/cs","Goodwill A/c Dr. To Kabir's and Leena's Capital A/cs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: if the new partner pays the premium for goodwill to the old partners privately, the money never passes through the firm.\nSo no entry is recorded in the firm's books.\nOption (1) is the entry only when the premium is paid through the firm, which is the usual case.\nOptions (3) and (4) are adjustment entries that are not needed here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Accountancy · Admission of a Partner","difficulty":"easy"}]});
